  'Clamminess': {
    'id': 9825,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMMINESS</b>, <i>n.</i> [See <a href="dict://Clammy">Clammy</a>.] The state of being viscous; viscosity; stickiness; tenacity of a soft substance.',
  },
  'Clammy': {
    'id': 9826,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMMY</b>, <i>a.</i> [See <a href="dict://Clam">Clam</a>.] Thick, viscous, adhesive; soft and sticky; glutinous; tenacious; as, bodies clammy and cleaving.<br><br>Cold sweat, in clammy drops, his limbs oer-spread.',
  },
  'Clamor': {
    'id': 9827,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMOR</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A great outcry; noise; exclamation; vociferation, made by a loud human voice continued or repeated, or by a multitude of voices. It often expresses complaint and urgent demand.<br><br>2. Figuratively, loud and continued noise, as of a river or other inanimate things.<br><br><b>CLAMOR</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To stun with noise.<br><br>To clamor bells, is to multiply the strokes.<br><br><b>CLAMOR</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To utter loud sounds, or outcries; to talk loud; to utter loud voices repeatedly; to vociferate, as an individual; to utter loud voices, as a multitude; to complain; to make importunate demands.<br><br>Those who most loudly clamor for liberty do not most liberally grant it.<br><br>Glamor your tongues in Shakespeare, if intended to mean, stop from noise, is not English. Perhaps the word was clam, or intended for a derivative.',
  },
  'Clamorer': {
    'id': 9828,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMORER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who clamors.',
  },
  'Clamoring': {
    'id': 9829,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMORING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Uttering and repeating loud words; making a great and continued noise; particularly in complaint or importunate demands.',
  },
  'Clamorous': {
    'id': 9830,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMOROUS</b>, <i>a.</i> Speaking and repeating loud words; noisy; vociferous; loud; turbulent.',
  },
  'Clamorously': {
    'id': 9831,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMOROUSLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With loud noise, or words.',
  },
  'Clamorousness': {
    'id': 9832,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMOROUSNESS</b>, <i>n.</i> The state or quality of being loud or noisy.',
  },
  'Clamp': {
    'id': 9833,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMP</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. In general, something that fastens or binds; a piece of timber or of iron, used to fasten work together; or a particular manner of uniting work by letting boards into each other.<br><br>2. In ship-building, a thick plank on the inner part of a ships side, used to sustain the ends of the beams.<br><br>3. A smooth crooked plate of iron forelocked on the trunnions of a cannon to keep it fast to the carriage. Clamps are also used to strengthen masts, and to fasten the masts and bowsprits of small vessels and of boats.<br><br>4. A pile of bricks laid up for burning, in which the end of one brick is laid over another, and a space is left between the bricks for the fire to ascend.<br><br>Clamp-irons, irons used at the ends of fires to keep the fuel from falling.<br><br>Clamp-nails, nails used to fasten on clamps in ships.<br><br><b>CLAMP</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To fasten with clamps.<br><br>2. In joinery, to fit a piece of board with the grain, to the end of another piece of board across the grain; as, to clamp a table to prevent its warping.',
  },
  'Clamped': {
    'id': 9834,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMPED</b>, <i>pp.</i> United or strengthened with a clamp.',
  },
  'Clamping': {
    'id': 9835,
    'definition': '<b>CLAMPING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Fastening or strengthening with a clamp.',
  },
  'Clan': {
    'id': 9836,
    'definition': '<b>CLAN</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A race; a family; a tribe. Hence, an association of persons under a chieftain.<br><br>2. In contempt, a sect, society, or body of persons closely united by some common interest or pursuit.<br><br>Note. In Russ. Kolieno signifies a knee, and a family, race or tribe. Irish glun, the knee, and a generation.',
  },
  'Clancular': {
    'id': 9837,
    'definition': '<b>CLANCULAR</b>, <i>a.</i> Clandestine; secret; private; concealed.',
  },
  'Clancularly': {
