  'Conduplication': {
    'id': 11366,
    'definition': '<b>CONDUPLICATION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L.] A doubling; a duplicate.',
  },
  'Condyl': {
    'id': 11367,
    'definition': '<b>CONDYL</b>, <i>n.</i> [L., Gr.] A protuberance on the end of a bone; a knot, or foint; a knuckle.',
  },
  'Condyloid': {
    'id': 11368,
    'definition': '<b>CONDYLOID</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr., and form.] The condyloid process is the posterior protuberance at the extremities of the under jaw; an oblong rounded head, which is received into the fossa of the temporal bone, forming a movable articulation. The anterior is called the coronoid process.<br><br><b>CONDYLOID</b>, <i>n.</i> The apophysis of a bone; the projecting soft end, or process of a bone.',
  },
  'Cone': {
    'id': 11369,
    'definition': '<b>CONE</b>, <i>n.</i> [It coincides in radical sense with the root of can and begin.]<br><br>1. A solid body or figure having a circle for its base, and its top terminated in a point or vertex, like a sugar loaf.<br><br>2. In botany, the conical fruit of several evergreen trees, as of the pine, fir, cedar, and cypress. It is composed of woody scales, usually opening , and has a seed at the base of each scale.<br><br>A cone of rays, in optics, includes all the rays of light which proceed from a radiant point and fall upon the surface of a glass.<br><br>A right cone, is when its axis is perpendicular to its base, and its sides equal. It is formed by the revolution of a right-angle plane triangle about one of its sides.<br><br>A scalene cone, is when its axis is inclined to its base and its sides unequal.',
  },
  'Conepate': {
    'id': 11370,
    'definition': '<b>CONEPATE, CONEPATL</b>, <i>n.</i> An animal of the weasel kind in America, resembling the pole-cat in form and size, and its fetid stench.',
