    'definition': '<b>DEICIDE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. God and to slay.]<br><br>1. The act of putting to death Jesus Christ, our Savior.<br><br>2. One concerned in putting Christ to death.',
  },
  'Deific': {
    'id': 14477,
    'definition': '<b>DEIFIC</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. To make.]<br><br>1. Divine; pertaining to the gods.<br><br>2. Making divine.',
  },
  'Deification': {
    'id': 14478,
    'definition': '<b>DEIFICATION</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of deifying; the act of exalting to the rank of, or enrolling among the heathen deities.',
  },
  'Deified': {
    'id': 14479,
    'definition': '<b>DEIFIED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Exalted or ranked among the gods; regarded or praised as divine.',
  },
  'Deifier': {
    'id': 14480,
    'definition': '<b>DEIFIER</b>, <i>n.</i> One that deifies.',
  },
  'Deiform': {
    'id': 14481,
    'definition': '<b>DEIFORM</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. A god, and form.] Like a god; of a godlike form.<br><br>These souls exhibit a deiform power.',
  },
  'Deify': {
    'id': 14482,
    'definition': '<b>DEIFY</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. A god, and to make.]<br><br>1. To make a god; to exalt to the rank of a heathen deity; to enroll among the deities; as, Julius Cesar was deified.<br><br>2. To exalt into an object of worship; to treat as an object of supreme regard; as a covetous man deifies his treasures.<br><br>3. To exalt to a deity in estimation; to reverence or praise as a deity.<br><br>The pope was formerly extolled and deified by his votaries.',
  },
  'Deifying': {
    'id': 14483,
    'definition': '<b>DEIFYING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Exalting to the rank of a deity; treating as divine.',
  },
  'Deign': {
    'id': 14484,
    'definition': '<b>DEIGN</b>, <i>v.i.</i> Dane. To think worthy; to vouchsafe; to condescend.<br><br>O deign to visit our foraken seats.<br><br><b>DEIGN</b>, <i>v.t.</i> dane. To grant or allow; to condescend to give to.<br><br>Nor would we deign him burial of his men.',
  },
  'Deigning': {
    'id': 14485,
    'definition': '<b>DEIGNING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Daning. Vouchsafing; thinking worthy.',
  },
  'Deintgrate': {
    'id': 14486,
    'definition': '<b>DEINTGRATE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To disintegrate.',
  },
  'Deiparous': {
    'id': 14487,
    'definition': '<b>DEIPAROUS</b>, <i>a.</i> Bearing or bringing forth a god; an epithet applied the Virgin Mary.',
  },
  'Deipnosophist': {
    'id': 14488,
    'definition': '<b>DEIPNOSOPHIST</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. A feast; a sophist.] One of an ancient sect of philosophers, who were famous for their learned conversation at meals.',
  },
  'Deism': {
    'id': 14489,
    'definition': '<b>DEISM</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. God.] The doctrine or creed of a deist; the belief or system of religious opinions of those who acknowledge the existence of one God, but deny revelation: or deism is the belief in natural religion only, or those truths, in doctrine and practice, which man is to discover by the light of reason, independent and exclusive of any revelation from God. Hence deism implies infidelity or a disbelief in the divine origin of the scriptures.<br><br>The view which the rising greatness of our country presents to my eyes, is greatly tarnished by the general prevalence of deism, which, with me, is but another name for vice and depravity. P. Henry, Wirys Sketches.',
  },
  'Deist': {
    'id': 14490,
    'definition': '<b>DEIST</b>, <i>n.</i> One who believes in the existence of a God, but denies revealed religion, but follows the light of nature and reason, as his only guides in doctrine and practice; a freethinker.',
  },
  'Deistic': {
    'id': 14491,
    'definition': '<b>DEISTIC,<br><br>DEISTICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to deism or to deists; embracing deism, as a deistical writer; or containing deism, as a deistical book.',
  },
  'Deity': {
    'id': 14492,
    'definition': '<b>DEITY</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. Godhead; divinity; the nature and essence of the Supreme Being; as, the deity of the Supreme Being is manifest in his works.<br><br>2. God; the Supreme Being, or infinite self-existing Spirit.<br><br>3. A fabulous god or goddess; a superior being supposed, by heathen nations, to exist, and to preside over particular departments of nature; as Jupiter, Juno, Apollo, Diana, &c.<br><br>4. The supposed divinity or divine qualities of a pagan god.',
  },
  'Deject': {
    'id': 14493,
    'definition': '<b>DEJECT</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. To throw.]<br><br>1. To cast down; usually, to cast down the countenance; to cause to fall with grief; to make to look sad or grieved, or to express discouragement.<br><br>But gloomy were his eyes, dejected was his face.<br><br>2. To depress the spirits; to sink; to dispirit; to discourage; to dishearten.<br><br>Nor think to die dejects my lofty mind.<br><br><b>DEJECT</b>, <i>a.</i> Cast down; low-spirited.',
  },
  'Dejected': {
    'id': 14494,
    'definition': '<b>DEJECTED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Cast down; depressed; grieved; discouraged.',
  },
  'Dejectedly': {
