    'definition': '<b>EMPUZ\'ZLE</b>. [See <a href="dict://Puzzle">Puzzle</a>.]',
  },
  'Empyreal': {
    'id': 18557,
    'definition': '<b>EMPYR\'EAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. empyroeus; from Gr. fire.]<br><br>1. Formed of pure fire or light; refined beyond aerial substance; pertaining to the highest and purest region of heaven.<br><br>Go, soar with Plato to the empyreal sphere.<br><br>2. Pure; vital; dephlogisticated; an epithet given to the air, or rather gas, now called oxygen.',
  },
  'Empyrean': {
    'id': 18558,
    'definition': '<b>EMPYRE\'AN</b>, <i>a.</i> Empyreal.<br><br><b>EMPYRE\'AN</b>, <i>n.</i> [See <a href="dict://Empyreal">Empyreal</a>.] The highest heaven, where the pure element of fire has been supposed to subsist.<br><br>The empyrean rung<br><br>With halleluiahs.',
  },
  'Empyreuma': {
    'id': 18559,
    'definition': '<b>EMPYREU\'MA</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. fire.] In chimistry, a disagreeable smell produced from burnt oils, in distillations of animal and vegetable substances.',
  },
  'Empyreumatic': {
    'id': 18560,
    'definition': '<b>EMPYREUMAT\'IC<br><br>EMPYREUMAT\'ICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> Having the taste or smell of burnt oil, or of burning animal and vegetable substances.',
  },
  'Empyrical': {
    'id': 18561,
    'definition': '<b>EMPYR\'ICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> Containing the combustible principle of coal.',
  },
  'Empyrosis': {
    'id': 18562,
    'definition': '<b>EMPYRO\'SIS</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. to burn.] a general fire; a conflagration. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Emrods': {
    'id': 18563,
    'definition': '<b>EMRODS</b>. [See <a href="dict://Emerods">Emerods</a>.]',
  },
  'Emu': {
    'id': 18564,
    'definition': '<b>E\'MU</b>, <i>n.</i> A large fowl of S. America, with wings unfit for flight.<br><br>This name properly belongs to the Cassowary, but has been erroneously applied, by the Brazilians, to the Rhea or S. American ostrich.',
  },
  'Emulate': {
    'id': 18565,
    'definition': '<b>EM\'ULATE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. oemulor; Gr. strife, contest.]<br><br>1. To strive to equal or excel, in qualities or actions; to imitate, with a view to equal or excel; to vie with; to rival. Learn early to emulate the good and the great. Emulate the virtues and shun the vices of distinguished men.<br><br>2. To be equal to.<br><br>Thy eye would emulate the diamond.<br><br>3. To imitate; to resemble. [Unusual.]<br><br>Convulsion emulating the motion of laughter.<br><br><b>EM\'ULATE</b>, <i>a.</i> Ambitious. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Emulated': {
    'id': 18566,
    'definition': '<b>EM\'ULATED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Rivaled; imitated.',
  },
  'Emulating': {
    'id': 18567,
    'definition': '<b>EM\'ULATING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Rivaling; attempting to equal or excel; imitating; resembling.',
  },
  'Emulation': {
    'id': 18568,
    'definition': '<b>EMULA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of attempting to equal or excel in qualities or actions; rivalry; desire of superiority, attended with effort to attain to it; generally in a good sense, or an attempt to equal or excel others in that which is praise-worthy, without the desire of depressing others. Rom 11. In a bad sense, a striving to equal or do more than others to obtain carnal favors or honors. Val.5.<br><br>1. An ardor kindled by the praise-worthy examples of others, inciting to imitate them, or to equal or excel them.<br><br>A noble emulation heats your breast.<br><br>2. Contest; contention; strife; competition; rivalry accompanied with a desire of depressing another.<br><br>Such factious emulations shall arise.',
  },
  'Emulative': {
    'id': 18569,
    'definition': '<b>EM\'ULATIVE</b>, <i>a.</i> Inclined to emulation; rivaling; disposed to competition.',
  },
  'Emulator': {
    'id': 18570,
    'definition': '<b>EM\'ULATOR</b>, <i>,</i> n. One who emulates; a rival; a competitor.',
  },
  'Emulatress': {
    'id': 18571,
    'definition': '<b>EM\'ULATRESS</b>, <i>n.</i> A female who emulates another.',
  },
  'Emule': {
    'id': 18572,
    'definition': '<b>EMU\'LE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To emulate. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Emulgent': {
    'id': 18573,
    'definition': '<b>EMULG\'ENT</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. emulgeo; e and mulgeo, to milk out.]<br><br>Milking or draining out. In anatomy, the emulgent or renal arteries are those which supply the kidneys with blood, being sometimes single, sometimes double. The emulgent veins return the blood, after the urine is secreted. This the ancients considered as a milking or straining of the serum whence the name.<br><br><b>EMULG\'ENT</b>, <i>n.</i> An emulgent vessel.',
  },
  'Emulous': {
    'id': 18574,
    'definition': '<b>EM\'ULOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. oemulus.] Desirous or eager to imitate, equal or excel another; desirous of like excellence with another; with of; as emulous of another\'s example or virtues.<br><br>1. Rivaling; engaged in competition; as emulous Carthage.<br><br>2. Factious; contentious.',
  },
  'Emulously': {
    'id': 18575,
    'definition': '<b>EM\'ULOUSLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With desire of equaling or excelling another.',
  },
  'Emulsion': {
    'id': 18576,
    'definition': '<b>EMUL\'SION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. emulsus, emulgeo, to milk out.]<br><br>A soft liquid remedy of a color and consistence resembling milk; any milk-like mixture prepared by uniting oil and water, by means of another substance, saccharine or mucilaginous.',
  },
  'Emulsive': {
    'id': 18577,
    'definition': '<b>EMUL\'SIVE</b>, <i>a.</i> Softening; milk-like.<br><br>1. Producing or yielding a milk-like substance; as emulsive acids.',
  },
  'Emunctory': {
    'id': 18578,
    'definition': '<b>EMUNC\'TORY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. emunctorium, from emunctus, emungo, to wipe, to cleanse.] In anatomy, any part of the body which serves to carry off excrementitious matter; a secretory gland; an excretory duct.<br><br>The kidneys and skin are called the common emunctories.',
  },
  'Emuscation': {
    'id': 18579,
    'definition': '<b>EMUSCA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. emuscor.] A freeing from moss. [Not much used.]',
  },
  'En': {
    'id': 18580,
    'definition': '<b>EN</b>, <i>a</i> prefix to many English words, chiefly borrowed from the French. In coincides with the Latin, in, Gr., and some English words are written indifferently with en or in. For the ease of pronunciation, it is changed to em, particularly before a labial, as in employ, empower.<br><br>En was formerly a plural termination of nouns and of verbs, as in housen, escapen. It is retained in oxen and children. It is also still used as the termination of some verbs, as in hearken, from the Saxon infinitive.',
  },
  'Enable': {
    'id': 18581,
    'definition': '<b>ENABLE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [Norm. enhabler; en and hable, able. See <a href="dict://Able">Able</a>.]<br><br>1. To make able; to supply with power,physical or moral; to furnish with sufficient power or ability. By strength a man is enabled to work. Learning and industry enable men to investigate the laws of nature. Fortitude enables us to bear pain without murmuring.<br><br>2. To supply with means. Wealth enables men to be charitable, or to live in luxury.<br><br>3. To furnish with legal ability or competency; to authorize. The law enables us to dispose of our property by will.<br><br>4. To furnish with competent knowledge or skill, and in general, with adequate means.',
  },
  'Enabled': {
    'id': 18582,
    'definition': '<b>ENA\'BLED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Supplied with sufficient power, physical, moral or legal.',
  },
  'Enablement': {
    'id': 18583,
    'definition': '<b>ENA\'BLEMENT</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of enabling; ability.',
  },
  'Enabling': {
    'id': 18584,
    'definition': '<b>ENA\'BLING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Giving power to; supplying with sufficient power, ability or means; authorizing.',
  },
  'Enact': {
    'id': 18585,
    'definition': '<b>ENACT\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [en and act.] To make, as a law; to pass, as a bill into a law; to perform the last act of a legislature to a bill, giving it validity as a law; to give legislative sanction to a bill.<br><br>Shall this bill pass to be enacted?<br><br>1. To decree; to establish as the will of the supreme power.<br><br>2. To act; to perform; to effect. [Not used.]<br><br>3. To represent inaction. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Enacted': {
    'id': 18586,
    'definition': '<b>ENACT\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Passed into a law; sanctioned as a law, by legislative authority.',
  },
  'Enacting': {
    'id': 18587,
    'definition': '<b>ENACT\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Passing into a law; giving legislative sanction to a bill, and establishing it as a law.<br><br>1. Giving legislative forms and sanction; as the enacting clause of a bill.',
  },
  'Enactment': {
    'id': 18588,
    'definition': '<b>ENACT\'MENT</b>, <i>n.</i> The passing of a bill into a law; the act of voting, decreeing and giving validity to a law.',
  },
  'Enactor': {
    'id': 18589,
    'definition': '<b>ENACT\'OR</b>, <i>n.</i> One who enacts or passes a law; one who decrees or establishes, as a law.<br><br>1. One who performs any thing. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Enacture': {
    'id': 18590,
    'definition': '<b>ENAC\'TURE</b>, <i>n.</i> Purpose. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Enallage': {
    'id': 18591,
    'definition': '<b>ENAL\'LAGE</b>, <i>n.</i> enal\'lajy. [Gr. change.]<br><br>A figure, in grammar, by which some change is made in the common mode of speech, or when one word is substituted for another; as exercitus victor, for victoriousus; scelus, for scelestus.',
  },
  'Enambush': {
    'id': 18592,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'BUSH</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [en and ambush.] To hide in ambush.<br><br>1. To ambush.',
  },
  'Enambushed': {
    'id': 18593,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'BUSHED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Concealed in ambush, or with hostile intention; ambushed.',
  },
  'Enamel': {
    'id': 18594,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'EL</b>, <i>n.</i><br><br>1. In mineralogy, a substance imperfectly vitrified, or matter in which the granular appearance is destroyed, and having a vitreous gloss.<br><br>In the arts, a substance of the nature of glass, differing from it by a greater degree of fusibility or opacity.<br><br>Enamels have for their basis a pure crystal glass or frit, ground with a fine oxyd of lead and tin. These baked together are the matter of enamels, and the color is varied by adding other substances, Oxyd of gold gives a red color; that of copper, a green; manganese, a violet; cobalt, a blue; and iron, a fine black.<br><br>2. That which is enameled; a smooth, glossy surface of various colors, resembling enamel.<br><br>3. In anatomy, the smooth hard substance which covers the crown of a tooth.<br><br><b>ENAM\'EL</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To lay enamel on a metal, as on gold, silver, copper, &c.<br><br>1. To paint in enamel.<br><br>2. To form a glossy surface like enamel.',
  },
  'Enamelar': {
    'id': 18595,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'ELAR</b>, <i>a.</i> Consisting of enamel; resembling enamel; smooth; glossy.',
  },
  'Enameled': {
    'id': 18596,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'ELED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Overlaid with enamel; adorned with any thing resembling enamel.',
  },
  'Enameler': {
    'id': 18597,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'ELER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who enamels; one whose occupation is to lay enamels, or inlay colors.',
  },
  'Enameling': {
    'id': 18598,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'ELING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Laying enamel.<br><br><b>ENAM\'ELING</b>, <i>n.</i> The act or art of laying enamels.',
  },
  'Enamor': {
    'id': 18599,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'OR</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. amor, love.] To inflame with love; to charm; to captivate; with of before the person or thing; as, to be enamored of a lady; to be enamored of books or science.<br><br>[But it is now followed by with.]',
  },
  'Enamorado': {
    'id': 18600,
    'definition': '<b>ENAMORA\'DO</b>, <i>n.</i> One deeply in love.',
  },
  'Enamored': {
    'id': 18601,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'ORED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Inflamed with love; charmed; delighted.',
  },
  'Enamoring': {
    'id': 18602,
    'definition': '<b>ENAM\'ORING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Inflaming with love; charming; captivating.',
  },
  'Enarmed': {
    'id': 18603,
    'definition': '<b>EN`ARMED</b>, <i>a.</i> In heraldry, having arms, that is, horns, hoofs, &c. of a different color from that of the body.',
  },
  'Enarration': {
    'id': 18604,
    'definition': '<b>ENARRA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. enarro,narro, to relate.] Recital; relation; account; exposition. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Enarthrosis': {
    'id': 18605,
    'definition': '<b>ENARTHRO\'SIS</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. a joint.] In anatomy, that species of articulation which consists in the insertion of the round end of a bone in the cup-like cavity of another, forming a movable joint; the ball and socket.',
  },
  'Enate': {
    'id': 18606,
    'definition': '<b>ENA\'TE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. enatus.] Growing out.',
  },
  'Enaunter': {
    'id': 18607,
    'definition': '<b>ENAUN\'TER</b>, <i>adv.</i> Lest that.',
  },
  'Encage': {
    'id': 18608,
    'definition': '<b>ENCA\'GE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [from cage.] To shut up or confine in a cage; to coop.',
  },
  'Encaged': {
    'id': 18609,
    'definition': '<b>ENCA\'GED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Shut up or confined in a cage.',
  },
  'Encaging': {
    'id': 18610,
    'definition': '<b>ENCA\'GING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Cooping; confining in a cage.',
  },
  'Encamp': {
    'id': 18611,
    'definition': '<b>ENCAMP\'</b>, <i>v.i.</i> [from camp.] To pitch tents or form huts, as an army; to halt on a march, spread tents and remain for a night or for a longer time, as an army or company.<br><br>They encamped in Etham. Exo 13.<br><br>The Levites shall encamp about the tabernacle. Num 1.<br><br>1. To pitch tents for the purpose of a siege; to besiege.<br><br>Encamp against the city and take it. 2 Sam 7.<br><br><b>ENCAMP\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To form into a camp; to place a marching army or company in a temporary habitation or quarters.',
  },
  'Encamped': {
    'id': 18612,
    'definition': '<b>ENCAMP\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Settled in tents or huts for lodging or temporary habitation.',
  },
  'Encamping': {
    'id': 18613,
    'definition': '<b>ENCAMP\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Pitching tents or forming huts, for a temporary lodging or rest.',
  },
  'Encampment': {
    'id': 18614,
    'definition': '<b>ENCAMP\'MENT</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of pitching tents or forming huts, as an army or traveling company, for temporary lodging or rest.<br><br>1. The place where an army or company is encamped; a camp; a regular order of tents or huts for the accommodation of an army or troop.',
  },
  'Encanker': {
    'id': 18615,
    'definition': '<b>ENCANK\'ER</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To corrode; to canker.',
  },
  'Encase': {
    'id': 18616,
    'definition': '<b>ENCA\'SE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To inclose or confine in a case or cover.',
  },
  'Encaustic': {
    'id': 18617,
    'definition': '<b>ENCAUS\'TIC</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr. caustic, to burn.] Pertaining to the art of enameling, and to painting in burnt wax. Encaustic painting, is a method in which wax is employed to give a gloss to colors.<br><br><b>ENCAUS\'TIC</b>, <i>n.</i> Enamel or enameling.<br><br>1. The method of painting in burnt wax.',
  },
  'Encave': {
    'id': 18618,
    'definition': '<b>ENCA\'VE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [from cave.] To hide in a cave or recess.',
  },
  'Enceint': {
    'id': 18619,
    'definition': '<b>ENCE\'INT</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. cingo, to gird.] In fortification, inclosure; the wall or rampart which surrounds a place, sometimes composed of bastions and curtains. It is sometimes only flanked by round or square towers, which is called a Roman wall.<br><br><b>ENCE\'INT</b>, <i>a.</i> In law, pregnant;; with child.',
  },
  'Enchafe': {
    'id': 18620,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'FE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [en and chafe.] To chafe or fret; to provoke; to enrage; to irritate. [See <a href="dict://Chafe">Chafe</a>.]',
  },
  'Enchafed': {
    'id': 18621,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'FED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Chafed; irritated; enraged.',
  },
  'Enchafing': {
    'id': 18622,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'FING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Chafing; fretting; enraging.',
  },
  'Enchain': {
    'id': 18623,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'IN</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To fasten with a chain; to bind or hold in chains; to hold in bondage.<br><br>2. To hold fast; to restrain; to confine.<br><br>3. To link together; to connect.',
  },
  'Enchained': {
    'id': 18624,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'INED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Fastened with a chain; held in bondage; held fast; restrained; confined.',
  },
  'Enchaining': {
    'id': 18625,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'INING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Making fast with a chain; binding; holding in chains; confining.',
  },
  'Enchant': {
    'id': 18626,
    'definition': '<b>ENCH`ANT</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. incanto; in and canto, to sing. See <a href="dict://Chant">Chant</a> and Cant.]<br><br>1. To practice sorcery or witchcraft on any thing; to give efficacy to any thing by songs of sorcery, or fascination.<br><br>And now about the cauldron sing,<br><br>Like elves and fairies in a ring,<br><br>Enchanting all that you put in.<br><br>2. To subdue by charms or spells.<br><br>3. To delight to the highest degree; to charm; to ravish with pleasure; as,the description enchants me; we were enchanted with the music.',
  },
  'Enchanted': {
    'id': 18627,
    'definition': '<b>ENCH`ANTED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Affected by sorcery; fascinated; subdued by charms; delighted beyond measure.<br><br>1. Inhabited or possessed by elves, witches, or other imaginary mischievous spirits; as an enchanted castle.',
  },
  'Enchanter': {
    'id': 18628,
    'definition': '<b>ENCH`ANTER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who enchants; a sorcerer or magician; one who has spirits or demons at his command; one who practices enchantment, or pretends to perform surprising things by the agency of demons.<br><br>1. One who charms or delights.<br><br>Enchanter\'s nightshade, a genus of plants, the Circaea.',
  },
  'Enchanting': {
    'id': 18629,
    'definition': '<b>ENCH`ANTING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Affecting with sorcery, charms or spells.<br><br>1. Delighting highly; ravishing with delight; charming.<br><br>2. Charming; delighting; ravishing; as an enchanting voice; an enchanting face.<br><br>Simplicity in manners has an enchanting effect.',
  },
  'Enchantingly': {
    'id': 18630,
    'definition': '<b>ENCH`ANTINGLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With the power of enchantment; in a manner to delight or charm; as, the lady sings enchantingly.',
  },
  'Enchantment': {
    'id': 18631,
    'definition': '<b>ENCH`ANTMENT</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of producing certain wonderful effects by the invocation or aid of demons, or the agency of certain supposed spirits; the use of magic arts, spells or charms; incantation.<br><br>The magicians of Egypt did so with their enchantments. Exo 7.<br><br>1. Irresistible influence; overpowering influence of delight.<br><br>The warmth of fancy--which holds the heart of a reader under the strongest enchantment.',
  },
  'Enchantress': {
    'id': 18632,
    'definition': '<b>ENCH`ANTRESS</b>, <i>n.</i> A sorceress; a woman who pretends to effect wonderful things by the aid of demons; one who pretends to practice magic.<br><br>1. A woman whose beauty or excellencies give irresistible influence.<br><br>From this enchantress all these ills are come.',
  },
  'Encharge': {
    'id': 18633,
    'definition': '<b>ENCH`ARGE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To give in charge or trust. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Enchase': {
    'id': 18634,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'SE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [Eng. a case.]<br><br>1. To infix or inclose in another body so as to be held fast, but not concealed.<br><br>2. Technically, to adorn by embossed work; to enrich or beautify any work in metal,by some design or figure in low relief, as a watch case.<br><br>3. To adorn by being fixed on it.<br><br>To drink in bowls which glittering gems enchase.<br><br>4. To mark by incision.<br><br>5. To delineate.',
  },
  'Enchased': {
    'id': 18635,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'SED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Enclosed as in a frame or in another body; adorned with embossed work.',
  },
  'Enchasing': {
    'id': 18636,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHA\'SING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Inclosing in another body; adorning with embossed work.',
  },
  'Encheason': {
    'id': 18637,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHE\'ASON</b>, <i>n.</i> Cause; occasion.',
  },
  'Enchiridion': {
    'id': 18638,
    'definition': '<b>ENCHIRID\'ION</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. the hand.] A manual; a book to be carried in the hand. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Encindered': {
    'id': 18639,
    'definition': '<b>ENCIN\'DERED</b>, <i>a.</i> Burnt to cinders.',
  },
  'Encircle': {
    'id': 18640,
    'definition': '<b>ENCIR\'CLE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> ensur\'cl. [from circle.]<br><br>1. To inclose or surround with a circle or ring, or with any thing in a circular form.<br><br>2. To encompass; to surround; to environ.<br><br>3. To embrace; as, to encircle one in the arms.',
  },
  'Encircled': {
    'id': 18641,
    'definition': '<b>ENCIR\'CLED</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Surrounded with a circle; encompassed; environed; embraced.',
  },
  'Encirclet': {
    'id': 18642,
    'definition': '<b>ENCIR\'CLET</b>, <i>n.</i> A circle; a ring.',
  },
  'Encircling': {
    'id': 18643,
    'definition': '<b>ENCIR\'CLING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Surrounding with a circle or ring; encompassing; embracing.',
  },
  'Enclitic': {
    'id': 18644,
    'definition': '<b>ENCLIT\'IC</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr. inclined; to incline.]<br><br>1. Leaning; inclining, or inclined. In grammar, an enclitic particle or word,is one which is so closely united to another as to seem to be a part of it; as que, ne, and ve, in virumque, nonne, aliusve.<br><br>2. Throwing back the accent upon the foregoing syllable.<br><br><b>ENCLIT\'IC</b>, <i>n.</i> A word which is joined to the end of another, as que, in virumque, which may vary the accent.<br><br>1. A particle or word that throws the accent or emphasis back upon the former syllable.',
  },
  'Enclitically': {
    'id': 18645,
    'definition': '<b>ENCLIT\'ICALLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> In an enclitic manner; by throwing the accent back.',
  },
  'Enclitics': {
    'id': 18646,
