  'Frequence': {
    'id': 22768,
    'definition': '<b>FRE\'QUENCE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. frequentia.] A crowd; a throng; a concourse; an assembly. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Frequency': {
    'id': 22769,
    'definition': '<b>FRE\'QUENCY</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A return or occurrence of a thing often repeated at short intervals. The frequency of crimes abates our horror at the commission; the frequency of capital punishments tends to destroy their proper effect.<br><br>2. A crowd; a throng. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Frequent': {
    'id': 22770,
    'definition': '<b>FRE\'QUENT</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. frequens.]<br><br>1. Often seen or done; often happening at short intervals; often repeated or occurring. We made frequent visits to the hospital.<br><br>2. Used often to practice any thing. He was frequent and loud in his declamations against the revolution.<br><br>3. Full; crowded; thronged. [Not used.]<br><br><b>FRE\'QUENT</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. frequento.]<br><br>To visit often; to resort to often or habitually. The man who frequents a dram-shop, an ale house, or a gaming table, is in the road to poverty, disgrace and ruin.<br><br>He frequented the court of Augustus.',
  },
  'Frequentable': {
