  'Full-Winged': {
    'id': 23061,
    'definition': '<b>FULL-WINGED</b>, <i>a.<br><br></i> 1. Having complete wings or large strong wings.<br><br>2. Ready for flight; eager.<br><br><b>FULL</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. fullo. Gr. that is, a crowd, a throng. foul and defile are probably of the same family.]<br><br>To thicken cloth in a mill. This is the primary sense: but in practice, to full is to mill; to make compact; or to scour, cleanse and thicken in a mill.',
  },
  'Fullage': {
    'id': 23062,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'AGE</b>, <i>n.</i> Money paid for fulling cloth.',
  },
  'Fulled': {
    'id': 23063,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Cleansed; thickened; made dense and firm in a mill.',
  },
  'Fuller': {
    'id': 23064,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'ER</b>, <i>n.</i> One whose occupation is to full cloth.',
  },
  'Fullers-Earth': {
    'id': 23065,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'ER\'S-EARTH</b>, <i>n.</i> A variety of clay, compact, but friable, unctuous to the touch, and of various colors, usually with a shade of green. It is useful in scouring and cleansing cloth, as it imbibes the grease and oil used in preparing wool.',
  },
  'Fullers-Thistle': {
    'id': 23066,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'ER\'S-THISTLE,<br><br>FULL\'ER\'S-WEED</b>, <i>n.</i> Teasel, a plant of the genus Dipsacus. The burs are used in dressing cloth.',
  },
  'Fullery': {
    'id': 23067,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'ERY</b>, <i>n.</i> The place or the works where the fulling of cloth is carried on.',
  },
  'Fulling': {
    'id': 23068,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Thickening cloth in a mill; making compact.<br><br><b>FULL\'ING</b>, <i>n.</i> The art or practice of thickening cloth and making it compact and firm in a mill, at the same time the cloth is cleansed of oily matter.',
  },
  'Fullingmill': {
    'id': 23069,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'INGMILL</b>, <i>n.</i> A mill for fulling cloth by means of pestles or stampers, which beat and press it to a close or compact state and cleanse it.',
  },
  'Fullness': {
    'id': 23070,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'NESS</b>, <i>n.</i> [from full.]<br><br>1. The state of being filled, so as to leave no part vacant.<br><br>2. The state of abounding or being in great plenty; abundance.<br><br>3. Completeness; the state of a thing in which nothing is wanted; perfection.<br><br>In thy presence is fullness of joy. Psa 16.<br><br>4. Repletion; satiety; as from intemperance.<br><br>5. Repletion of vessels; as fullness of blood.<br><br>6. Plenty; wealth; affluence.<br><br>7. Struggling perturbation; swelling; as the fullness of the heart.<br><br>8. Largeness; extent.<br><br>There wanted the fullness of a plot, and variety of characters to form it as it ought.<br><br>9. Loudness; force of sound, such as fills the ear.',
  },
  'Fullsome': {
    'id': 23071,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'SOME</b>, <i>a.</i> Gross; disgusting by plainness, grossness or excess; as fullsome flattery or praise.',
  },
  'Fullsomely': {
    'id': 23072,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'SOMELY</b>, <i>adv.</i> Grossly; with disgusting plainness or excess.',
  },
  'Fullsomeness': {
    'id': 23073,
    'definition': '<b>FULL\'SOMENESS</b>, <i>n.</i> Offensive grossness, as of praise.<br><br>[These are the senses of this word and the only senses used in New England, as far as my knowledge extends.]',
  },
  'Fully': {
    'id': 23074,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'LY</b>, <i>adv.<br><br></i> 1. Completely; entirely; without lack or defect; in a manner to give satisfaction; to the extent desired; as, to be fully persuaded of the truth of a proposition.<br><br>2. Completely; perfectly. Things partially known in this life will be hereafter fully disclosed.',
  },
  'Fulmar': {
    'id': 23075,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'MAR</b>, <i>n.</i><br><br>1. A fowl of the genus Procellaria, or petrel kind, larger than a gull, possessing the singular faculty of spouting from its bill a quantity of pure oil against its adversary. It is an inhabitant of the Hebrides; it feeds on the fat of whales, and when one of them is taken, will perch on it even when alive and pick out pieces of flesh.<br><br>2. The foulemart or fulimart. [See <a href="dict://Foumart">Foumart</a>.]',
  },
  'Fulminant': {
    'id': 23076,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'MINANT</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. fulminans.] Thundering.',
  },
  'Fulminate': {
    'id': 23077,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'MINATE</b>, <i>v.i.</i> [L. fulmino, from fulmen, thunder, from a root in Bl, which signifies to throw or to burst forth.]<br><br>1. To thunder.<br><br>2. To make a loud sudden noise, or a sudden sharp crack; to detonate; as fulminating gold.<br><br>3. To hurl papal thunder; to issue forth ecclesiastical censures, as the pope.<br><br><b>FUL\'MINATE</b>, <i>v.t.</i><br><br>1. To utter or send out, as a denunciation or censure; to send out, as a menace or censure by ecclesiastical authority.<br><br>2. To cause to explode.',
  },
  'Fulmination': {
    'id': 23078,
    'definition': '<b>FULMINA\'TION</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A thundering.<br><br>2. Denunciation of censure or threats, as by papal authority.<br><br>The fulminations from the Vatican were turned into ridicule.<br><br>3. The explosion of certain chimical preparations; detonation.',
  },
  'Fulminatory': {
    'id': 23079,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'MINATORY</b>, <i>a.</i> Thundering; striking terror.',
  },
  'Fulmine': {
    'id': 23080,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'MINE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To thunder. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Fulminic': {
    'id': 23081,
    'definition': '<b>FULMIN\'IC</b>, <i>a.</i> Fulminic acid, in chimistry, is a peculiar acid contained in fulminating silver.',
  },
  'Fulsome': {
    'id': 23082,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'SOME</b>, <i>a.</i><br><br>1. Nauseous; offensive.<br><br>He that brings fulsome objects to my view, with nauseous images my fancy fills.<br><br>2. Rank; offensive to the smell; as a rank and fulsome smell.<br><br>3. Lustful; as fulsome ewes.<br><br>4. Tending to obscenity; as a fulsome epigram.<br><br>These are the English definitions of fulsome, but I have never witnessed such applications of the word in the United States. It seems then that full and foul are radically the same word, the primary sense of which is stuffed, crowded, from the sense of putting on or in. In the United States, the compound fullsome takes its signification from full, in the sense of cloying or satiating, and in England, fulsome takes its predominant sense from foulness.',
  },
  'Fulsomely': {
    'id': 23083,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'SOMELY</b>, <i>adv.</i> Rankly; nauseously; obscenely.',
  },
  'Fulsomeness': {
    'id': 23084,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'SOMENESS</b>, <i>n.</i> Nauseousness; rank smell; obscenity.',
  },
  'Fulvid': {
    'id': 23085,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'VID</b>, <i>a.</i> [See <a href="dict://Fulvous,">Fulvous,</a> which is generally used.]',
  },
  'Fulvous': {
    'id': 23086,
    'definition': '<b>FUL\'VOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. fulvus.] Yellow; tawny; saffron-colored.',
  },
  'Fumado': {
    'id': 23087,
    'definition': '<b>FUMA\'DO</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fumus, smoke.] A smoked fish.',
  },
  'Fumatory': {
    'id': 23088,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MATORY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fumaria herba.]<br><br>A plant or genus of plants, called Fumaria, of several species.',
  },
  'Fumble': {
    'id': 23089,
    'definition': '<b>FUM\'BLE</b>, <i>v.i.<br><br></i> 1. To feel or grope about; to attempt awkwardly.<br><br>2. To grope about in perplexity; to seek awkwardly; as, to fumble for an excuse.<br><br>3. To handle much; to play childishly; to turn over and over.<br><br>I saw him fumble with the sheets, and play with flowers.<br><br><b>FUM\'BLE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To manage awkwardly; to crowd or tumble together.',
  },
  'Fumbler': {
    'id': 23090,
    'definition': '<b>FUM\'BLER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who gropes or manages awkwardly.',
  },
  'Fumbling': {
    'id': 23091,
    'definition': '<b>FUM\'BLING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Groping; managing awkwardly.',
  },
  'Fumblingly': {
    'id': 23092,
    'definition': '<b>FUM\'BLINGLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> In an awkward manner.',
  },
  'Fume': {
    'id': 23093,
    'definition': '<b>FUME</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fumus.]<br><br>1. Smoke; vapor from combustion, as from burning wood or tobacco.<br><br>2. Vapor; volatile matter ascending in a dense body.<br><br>3. Exhalation from the stomach; as the fumes of wine.<br><br>4. Rage; heat; as the fumes of passion.<br><br>5. Any thing unsubstantial or fleeting.<br><br>6. Idle conceit; vain imagination.<br><br><b>FUME</b>, <i>v.i.</i> [L. fumo.]<br><br>1. To smoke; to throw off vapor, as in combustion.<br><br>Where the golden altar fumed.<br><br>2. To yield vapor or visible exhalations.<br><br>Silenus lay, whose constant cups lay fuming to his brain.<br><br>3. To pass off in vapors.<br><br>Their parts are kept from fuming away by their fixity.<br><br>4. To be in a rage; to be hot with anger.<br><br>He fret, he fumes, he stares, he stamps the ground.<br><br><b>FUME</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To smoke; to dry in smoke.<br><br>2. To perfume,<br><br>She fumed the temples with an od\'rous flame.<br><br>3. To disperse or drive away in vapors.<br><br>The heat will fume away most of the scent.',
  },
  'Fumet': {
    'id': 23094,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MET</b>, <i>n.</i> The dung of deer.',
  },
  'Fumid': {
    'id': 23095,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MID</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. fumidus.] Smoky; vaporous.',
  },
  'Fumigate': {
    'id': 23096,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MIGATE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. fumigo.]<br><br>1. To smoke; to perfume.<br><br>2. To apply smoke to; to expose to smoke; as in chimistry, or in medicine by inhaling it, or in cleansing infected apartments.',
  },
  'Fumigated': {
    'id': 23097,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MIGATED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Smoked; exposed to smoke.',
  },
  'Fumigating': {
    'id': 23098,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MIGATING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Smoking; applying smoke to.',
  },
  'Fumigation': {
    'id': 23099,
    'definition': '<b>FUMIGA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fumigatio.]<br><br>1. The act of smoking or applying smoke, as in chimistry for softening a metal, or in the healing art by inhaling the smoke of certain substances. Expectoration is often assisted and sometimes ulcers of the lungs healed by fumigation. Fumigation is also used in cleansing infected rooms.<br><br>2. Vapors; scent raised by fire.',
  },
  'Fuming': {
    'id': 23100,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Smoking; emitting vapors; raging; fretting.',
  },
  'Fumingly': {
    'id': 23101,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MINGLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> Angrily; in a rage.',
  },
  'Fumish': {
    'id': 23102,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MISH</b>, <i>a.</i> Smoky; hot; choleric. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Fumiter': {
    'id': 23103,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MITER</b>, <i>n.</i> A plant.',
  },
  'Fumous': {
    'id': 23104,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'MOUS,<br><br>FU\'MY</b>, <i>a.</i> Producing fume; full of vapor.<br><br>From dice and wine the youth retir\'d to rest, and puffed the funny god from out his breast.',
  },
  'Fun': {
    'id': 23105,
    'definition': '<b>FUN</b>, <i>n.</i> Sport; vulgar merriment. A low word.',
  },
  'Fun-Gin': {
    'id': 23106,
    'definition': '<b>FUN-GIN</b>, <i>n.</i> The fleshy part of mushrooms, now considered as a peculiar vegetable principle.',
  },
  'Funambulatory': {
    'id': 23107,
    'definition': '<b>FUNAM\'BULATORY</b>, <i>a.</i> Performing like a rope dancer; narrow like the walk of a rope dancer.',
  },
  'Funambulist': {
    'id': 23108,
    'definition': '<b>FUNAM\'BULIST</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. funis, rope, and ambulo, to walk.] A rope walker or dancer.',
  },
  'Function': {
    'id': 23109,
    'definition': '<b>FUNC\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. functio, from fungor, to perform.]<br><br>1. In a general sense, the doing, executing or performing of any thing; discharge; performance; as the function of a calling or office. More generally,<br><br>2. Office or employment, or any duty or business belonging to a particular station or character, or required of a person in the station or character. Thus we speak of the functions of a chancellor, judge or bishop; the functions of a parent or guardian.<br><br>3. Trade; occupation. [Less proper.]<br><br>4. The office of any particular part of animal bodies; the peculiar or appropriate action of a member or part of the body, by which the animal economy is carried on. Thus we speak of the functions of the brain and nerves, of the heart, of the liver, of the muscles, &c.<br><br>5. Power; faculty, animal or intellectual.<br><br>As the mind opens, and its functions spread.<br><br>6. In mathematics, the function of a variable quantity, is any algebraic expression into which that quantity enters, mixed with other quantities that have invariable values.',
  },
  'Functionally': {
    'id': 23110,
    'definition': '<b>FUNC\'TIONALLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> By means of the functions.',
  },
  'Functionary': {
    'id': 23111,
    'definition': '<b>FUNC\'TIONARY</b>, <i>n.</i> One who holds an office or trust; as a public functionary; secular functionaries.',
  },
  'Fund': {
    'id': 23112,
    'definition': '<b>FUND</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fundus, ground bottom, foundation; connected with L. fundo, to found, the sense of which is to throw down, to set, to lay. Heb. to build. L. funda, a sling, a casting net or purse.]<br><br>1. A stock or capital; a sum of money appropriated as the foundation of some commercial or other operation, undertaken with a view to profit, and by means of which expenses and credit are supported. Thus the capital stock of a banking institution is called its fund; the joint stock of a commercial or manufacturing house constitutes its fund or funds; and hence the word is applied to the money which an individual may possess, or the means he can employ for carrying on any enterprise or operation. No prudent man undertakes an expensive business without funds.<br><br>2. Money lent to government, constituting a national debt; or the stock of a national debt. Thus we say, a man is interested in the funds or public funds, when he owns the stock or the evidences of the public debt; and the funds are said to rise or fall, when a given amount of that debt sells for more or less in the market.<br><br>3. Money or income destined to the payment of the interest of a debt.<br><br>4. A sinking fund is a sum of money appropriated to the purchase of the public stocks or the payment of the public debt.<br><br>5. A stock or capital to afford supplies of any kind; as a fund of wisdom or good sense; a fund of wit. Hence.<br><br>6. Abundance; ample stock or store.<br><br><b>FUND</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To provide and appropriate a fund or permanent revenue for the payment of the interest of; to make permanent provision of resources for discharging the annual interest of; as, to fund exchequer bills or government notes; to fund a national debt.<br><br>2. To place money in a fund.',
  },
  'Fundament': {
    'id': 23113,
    'definition': '<b>FUND\'AMENT</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fundamentum, from fundo, to set.]<br><br>1. The seat; the lower part of the body or of the intestinum rectum.<br><br>2. Foundation. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Fundamental': {
    'id': 23114,
    'definition': '<b>FUNDAMENT\'AL</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to the foundation or basis; serving for the foundation. Hence, essential; important; as a fundamental truth or principle; a fundamental law; a fundamental sound or chord in music.<br><br><b>FUNDAMENT\'AL</b>, <i>n.</i> A leading or primary principle, rule, law or article, which serves as the ground work of a system; essential part; as the fundamentals of the christian faith.',
  },
  'Fundamentally': {
    'id': 23115,
    'definition': '<b>FUNDAMENT\'ALLY</b>, <i>n.</i> Primarily; originally; essentially; at the foundation. All power is fundamentally in the citizens of a state.',
  },
  'Funded': {
    'id': 23116,
    'definition': '<b>FUND\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Furnished with funds for regular payment of the interest of.',
  },
  'Funding': {
    'id': 23117,
    'definition': '<b>FUND\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Providing funds for the payment of the interest of.',
  },
  'Funebrial': {
    'id': 23118,
    'definition': '<b>FUNE\'BRIAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. funebris.] Pertaining to funerals.',
  },
  'Funeral': {
    'id': 23119,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'NERAL</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. funus, from funale, a cord, a torch, from funis, a rope or cord, as torches were made of cords, and were used in burials among the Romans.]<br><br>1. Burial; the ceremony of buying a dead body; the solemnization of interment; obsequies.<br><br>2. The procession of persons attending the burial of the dead.<br><br>3. Burial; interment.<br><br><b>FU\'NERAL</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to burial; used at the interment of the dead; as funeral rites, honors or ceremonies; a funeral torch; funeral feast or games; funeral oration.',
  },
  'Funeration': {
    'id': 23120,
    'definition': '<b>FUNERA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> Solemnization of a funeral. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Funereal': {
    'id': 23121,
    'definition': '<b>FUNE\'REAL</b>, <i>a.<br><br></i> 1. Suiting a funeral; pertaining to burial.<br><br>2. Dark; dismal; mournful.',
  },
  'Fungate': {
    'id': 23122,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'GATE</b>, <i>n.</i> [from fungus.] A compound of fungic acid and a base.',
  },
  'Fungic': {
    'id': 23123,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'GIC</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to or obtained from mushrooms; as fungic acid.',
  },
  'Fungiform': {
    'id': 23124,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'GIFORM</b> a. [fungus and form.] In mineralogy, having a termination similar to the head of a fungus.',
  },
  'Fungite': {
    'id': 23125,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'GITE</b>, <i>n.</i> [from fungus.] A kind of fossil coral.',
  },
  'Fungosity': {
    'id': 23126,
    'definition': '<b>FUNGOS\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> Soft excrescence.',
  },
  'Fungous': {
    'id': 23127,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'GOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [See <a href="dict://Fungus">Fungus</a>.]<br><br>1. Like fungus or a mushroom; excrescent; spungy; soft.<br><br>2. Growing suddenly, but not substantial or durable.',
  },
  'Fungus': {
    'id': 23128,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'GUS</b>, <i>n.</i> [L.]<br><br>1. A mushroom, vulgarly called a toadstool. The Fungi constitute an order of plants of a peculiar organization and manner of growth. The word is also applied to excrescences on plants.<br><br>2. A spungy excrescence in animal bodies as proud flesh formed in wounds.<br><br>The term is particularly applied to any morbid excrescence, whether in wounds or arising spontaneously.',
  },
  'Funicle': {
    'id': 23129,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'NICLE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. faniculus, dim of funis, a cord.]<br><br>A small cord; a small ligature; a fiber.',
  },
  'Funicular': {
    'id': 23130,
    'definition': '<b>FUNIC\'ULAR</b>, <i>a.</i> Consisting of a small cord or fiber.',
  },
  'Funk': {
    'id': 23131,
    'definition': '<b>FUNK</b>, <i>n.</i> An offensive smell. [Vulgar.]',
  },
  'Funnel': {
    'id': 23132,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'NEL</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A passage or avenue for a fluid or flowing substance, particularly the shaft or hollow channel of a chimney through which smoke ascends.<br><br>2. A vessel for conveying fluids into close vessels; a kind of hollow cone with a pipe; a tunnel.',
  },
  'Funnelform': {
    'id': 23133,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'NELFORM,<br><br>FUN\'NELSHAPED</b>, <i>a.</i> Having the form of a funnel or inverted hollow cone.',
  },
  'Funny': {
    'id': 23134,
    'definition': '<b>FUN\'NY</b>, <i>a.</i> [from fun.] Droll; comical.<br><br><b>FUN\'NY</b>, <i>n.</i> A light boat.',
  },
  'Fur': {
    'id': 23135,
    'definition': '<b>FUR</b>, <i>n.</i><br><br>1. The short, fine, soft hair of certain animals, growing thick on the skin, and distinguished from the hair, which is longer and coarser. Fur is one of the most perfect non-conductors of heat, and serves to keep animals warm in cold climates.<br><br>2. The skins of certain wild animals with the fur; peltry; as a cargo of furs.<br><br>3. Strips of skin with fur, used on garments for lining or for ornament. Garments are lined or faced with fur.<br><br>4. Hair in general; a loose application of the word.<br><br>5. A coat of morbid matter collected on the tongue in persons affected with fever.<br><br><b>FUR</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To line, face or cover with fur; as a furred robe.<br><br>2. To cover with morbid matter, as the tongue.<br><br>3. To line with a board, as in carpentry.',
  },
  'Furacious': {
    'id': 23136,
    'definition': '<b>FURA\'CIOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. furax, from furer, to steal.]<br><br>Given to theft; inclined to steal; thievish. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Furacity': {
    'id': 23137,
    'definition': '<b>FURAC\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> Thievishness. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Furbelow': {
    'id': 23138,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'BELOW</b>, <i>n.</i> A piece of stuff plaited and puckered, on a gown or petticoat; a flounce; the plaited border of a petticoat or gown.<br><br><b>FUR\'BELOW</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To put on a furbelow; to furnish with an ornamental appendage of dress.',
  },
  'Furbish': {
    'id': 23139,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'BISH</b>, <i>v.t.</i><br><br>To rub or scour to brightness; to polish; to burnish; as, to furbish a sword or spear; to furbish arms.',
  },
  'Furbished': {
    'id': 23140,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'BISHED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Scoured to brightness; polished; burnished.',
  },
  'Furbisher': {
    'id': 23141,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'BISHER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who polishes or makes bright by rubbing; one who cleans.',
  },
  'Furbishing': {
    'id': 23142,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'BISHING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Rubbing to brightness; polishing.',
  },
  'Furcate': {
    'id': 23143,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'CATE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. furca, a fork.] Forked; branching like the prongs of a fork.',
  },
  'Furcation': {
    'id': 23144,
    'definition': '<b>FURCA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> A forking; a branching like the times of a fork.',
  },
  'Furdle': {
    'id': 23145,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'DLE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To draw up into a bundle. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Furfur': {
    'id': 23146,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'FUR</b>, <i>n.</i> [L.] Dandruff; scurf; scales like bran.',
  },
  'Furfuraceous': {
    'id': 23147,
    'definition': '<b>FURFURA\'CEOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. furfuraceus.] Scaly; branny; scurfy; like bran.',
  },
  'Furious': {
    'id': 23148,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'RIOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [See <a href="dict://Fury">Fury</a>.]<br><br>1. Rushing with impetuosity; moving with violence; as a furious stream; a furious wind or storm.<br><br>2. Raging; violent; transported with passion; as a furious animal.<br><br>3. Mad; phrenetic.',
  },
  'Furiously': {
    'id': 23149,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'RIOUSLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With impetuous motion or agitation; violently; vehemently; as, to run furiously; to attack one furiously.',
  },
  'Furiousness': {
    'id': 23150,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'RIOUSNESS</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. Impetuous motion or rushing; violent agitation.<br><br>2. Madness; phrensy; rage..',
  },
  'Furl': {
    'id': 23151,
    'definition': '<b>FURL</b>, <i>v.t.</i><br><br>To draw up; to contract; to wrap or roll a sail close to the yard, stay or mast, and fasten it by a gasket or cord.',
  },
  'Furled': {
    'id': 23152,
    'definition': '<b>FURL\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Wrapped and fastened to a yard, &c.',
  },
  'Furling': {
    'id': 23153,
    'definition': '<b>FURL\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Wrapping or rolling and fastening to a yard, &c.',
  },
  'Furlong': {
    'id': 23154,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'LONG</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> A measure of length; the eighth part of a mile; forty rods, poles or perches.',
  },
  'Furlow': {
    'id': 23155,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'LOW</b>, <i>n.</i> [See <a href="dict://Fare">Fare</a> and Leave.]<br><br>Leave of absence; a word used only in military affairs. Leave or license given by a commanding officer to an officer or soldier to be absent from service for a certain time.<br><br><b>FUR\'LOW</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To furnish with a furlow; to grant leave of absence to an officer or soldier.',
  },
  'Furmenty': {
    'id': 23156,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'MENTY</b>, <i>n.</i> [See <a href="dict://Frumenty">Frumenty</a>.]',
  },
  'Furnace': {
    'id': 23157,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'NACE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fornax, furnus, either from burning, or the sense is an arch.]<br><br>1. A place where a vehement fire and heat may be made and maintained, for melting ores or metals, &c. A furnace for casting cannon and other large operations is inclosed with walls through which a current of air is blown from a large bellows. In smaller operations a vessel is constructed with a chamber or cavity, with a door and a grate.<br><br>2. In scripture, a place of cruel bondage and affliction. Deu 4.<br><br>3. Grievous afflictions by which men are tried. Ezek 22.<br><br>4. A place of temporal torment. Dan 3.<br><br>5. Hell; the place of endless torment. Mat 13.<br><br><b>FUR\'NACE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To throw out sparks as a furnace.',
  },
  'Furniment': {
    'id': 23158,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'NIMENT</b>, <i>n.</i> Furniture. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Furnish': {
    'id': 23159,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'NISH</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [There is a close affinity, in sense and elements, between furnish, garnish, and the L. orno, which may have been forno or horno. We see in furlow, above the f is lost in three of the languages, and it may be so in orno. The primary sense is to put on, or to set on.]<br><br>1. To supply with any thing wanted or necessary; as, to furnish a family with provisions; to furnish arms for defense; to furnish a table; to furnish a library; to furnish one with money or implements.<br><br>2. To supply; to store; as, to furnish the mind with ideas; to furnish one with knowledge or principles.<br><br>3. To fit up; to supply with the proper goods, vessels or ornamental appendages; as, to furnish a house or a room.<br><br>4. To equip; to fit for an expedition; to supply.',
  },
  'Furnished': {
    'id': 23160,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'NISHED</b>, <i>a.</i> Supplied; garnished; fitted with necessaries.',
  },
  'Furnisher': {
    'id': 23161,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'NISHER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who supplies or fits out.',
  },
  'Furnishing': {
    'id': 23162,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'NISHING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Supplying; fitting; garnishing.',
  },
  'Furniture': {
    'id': 23163,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'NITURE</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. Goods, vessels, utensils and other appendages necessary or convenient for housekeeping; whatever is added to the interior of a house or apartment, for use or convenience.<br><br>2. Appendages; that which is added for use or ornament; as the earth with all its furniture.<br><br>3. Equipage; ornaments; decorations; in a very general sense.',
  },
  'Furred': {
    'id': 23164,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'RED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Lined or ornamented with fur; thickened by the addition of a board.',
  },
  'Furrier': {
    'id': 23165,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'RIER</b>, <i>n.</i> A dealer in furs; one who makes or sells muffs, tippets, &c.',
  },
  'Furriery': {
    'id': 23166,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'RIERY</b>, <i>n.</i> Furs in general.',
  },
  'Furring': {
    'id': 23167,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'RING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Lining or ornamenting with fur; lining with a board.',
  },
  'Furrow': {
    'id': 23168,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'ROW</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. to plow.]<br><br>1. A trench in the earth made by a plow.<br><br>2. A long narrow trench or channel in wood or metal; a groove.<br><br>3. A hollow made by wrinkles in the face.<br><br><b>FUR\'ROW</b>, <i>v.t.</i><br><br>1. To cut a furrow; to make furrows in; to plow.<br><br>2. To make long narrow channels or grooves in.<br><br>3. To cut; to make channels in; to plow; as, to furrow the deep.<br><br>4. To make hollows in by wrinkles. Sorrow furrows the brow.',
  },
  'Furrowfaced': {
    'id': 23169,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'ROWFACED</b>, <i>a.</i> Having a wrinkled or furrowed face.',
  },
  'Furrowweed': {
    'id': 23170,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'ROWWEED</b>, <i>n.</i> A weed growing on plowed land.',
  },
  'Furry': {
    'id': 23171,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'RY</b>, <i>a.</i> [from fur.]<br><br>1. Covered with fur; dressed in fur.<br><br>2. Consisting of fur or skins; as furry spoils.',
  },
  'Further': {
    'id': 23172,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'THER</b>, <i>a.<br><br></i> 1. More or most distant; as the further end of the field.<br><br>2. Additional. We have a further reason for this opinion. We have nothing further to suggest.<br><br>What further need have we of witnesses? Mat 26.<br><br><b>FUR\'THER</b>, <i>adv.</i> To a greater distance. He went further.<br><br><b>FUR\'THER</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> To help forward; to promote; to advance onward; to forward; hence, to help or assist.<br><br>This binds thee then to further my design.',
  },
  'Furtherance': {
    'id': 23173,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'THERANCE</b>, <i>n.</i> A helping forward; promotion; advancement.<br><br>I know that I shall abide and continue with you all, for your furtherance and joy of faith. Phil 1.',
  },
  'Furthered': {
    'id': 23174,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'THERED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Promoted; advanced.',
  },
  'Furtherer': {
    'id': 23175,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'THERER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who helps to advance; a promoter.',
  },
  'Furthermore': {
    'id': 23176,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'THERMORE</b>, <i>adv.</i> Moreover; besides; in addition to what has been said.',
  },
  'Furthest': {
    'id': 23177,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'THEST</b>, <i>a.</i> Most distant either in time or place.<br><br><b>FUR\'THEST</b>, <i>adv.</i> At the greatest distance.',
  },
  'Furtive': {
    'id': 23178,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'TIVE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. furtivus.]<br><br>Stolen; obtained by theft.',
  },
  'Furuncle': {
    'id': 23179,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'RUNCLE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. furunculus, furia, furo.]<br><br>A small tumor or boil, with inflammation and pain, arising under the skin in the adipose membrane.',
  },
  'Furwrought': {
    'id': 23180,
    'definition': '<b>FUR\'WROUGHT</b>, <i>a</i> fur\'raut. Made of fur.',
  },
  'Fury': {
    'id': 23181,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'RY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. furor, furia, furo, to rage.]<br><br>1. A violent rushing; impetuous motion; as the fury of the winds.<br><br>2. Rage; a storm of anger; madness; turbulence.<br><br>I do oppose my patience to his fury.<br><br>3. Enthusiasm; heat of the mind.<br><br>4. In mythology, a deity; a goddess of vengeance; hence, a stormy turbulent, violent woman.',
  },
  'Furylike': {
    'id': 23182,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'RYLIKE</b>, <i>a.</i> Raging; furious; violent.',
  },
  'Furz': {
    'id': 23183,
    'definition': '<b>FURZ</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> Gorse; whin; a thorny plant of the genus Ulex.',
  },
  'Furzy': {
    'id': 23184,
    'definition': '<b>FURZ\'Y</b>, <i>a.</i> Overgrown with furz; full of gorse.',
  },
  'Fuscite': {
    'id': 23185,
    'definition': '<b>FUS\'CITE</b>, <i>n.</i> A mineral of a grayish or greenish black color, found in Norway.',
  },
  'Fuscous': {
    'id': 23186,
    'definition': '<b>FUS\'COUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. fuscus.] Brown; of a dark color.',
  },
  'Fuse': {
    'id': 23187,
    'definition': '<b>FUSE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> s as z. [L. fundo, fusum, to pour out.]<br><br>To melt; to liquefy by heat; to render fluid; to dissolve.<br><br><b>FUSE</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To be melted; to be reduced from a solid to a fluid state by heat.',
  },
  'Fused': {
    'id': 23188,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'SED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Melted; liquefied.',
  },
  'Fusee': {
    'id': 23189,
    'definition': '<b>FUSEE\'</b>, <i>n.</i> s as z. [L. fusus, a spindle, from fundo, fudi, fusum.]<br><br>The cone or conical part of a watch or clock, round which is wound the chain or cord.<br><br><b>FUSEE\'</b>, <i>n.</i> s as z.<br><br>1. A small neat musket or firelock. But we now use fusil.<br><br>2. Fusee or fuse of a bomb or granade, a small pipe filled with combustible matter by which fire is communicated to the powder in the bomb; but as the matter burns slowly, time is given before the charge takes fire, for the bomb to reach its destination.<br><br>3. The track of a buck.',
  },
  'Fusibility': {
    'id': 23190,
    'definition': '<b>FUSIBIL\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> [See <a href="dict://fusible">fusible</a>.] The quality of being fusible, or of being convertible from a solid to a fluid state by heat.',
  },
  'Fusible': {
    'id': 23191,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'SIBLE</b>, <i>a</i> s as z. [L. fusus, from fundo.]<br><br>That may be melted or liquefied. The earths are found to be fusible.',
  },
  'Fusiform': {
    'id': 23192,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'SIFORM</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. fusus, a spindle,and form.]<br><br>Shaped like a spindle.',
  },
  'Fusil': {
    'id': 23193,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'SIL</b>, <i>a,</i> s as z. [L. fusilis, from fusus,fundo.]<br><br>1. Capable of being melted or rendered fluid by heat.<br><br>2. Running; flowing, as a liquid.<br><br><b>FU\'SIL</b>, <i>n.</i> s as z. [L. fusus, fundo.]<br><br>1. A light musket or firelock.<br><br>2. A bearing in heraldry of a rhomboidal figure, named from its shape, which resembles that of a spindle.',
  },
  'Fusileer': {
    'id': 23194,
    'definition': '<b>FUSILEE\'R</b>,n. [from fusil.] Properly, a soldier armed with a fusil;but in modern times, a soldier armed like others of the infantry, and distinguished by wearing a cap like a grenadier, but somewhat shorter.',
  },
  'Fusion': {
    'id': 23195,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'SION</b>, <i>n.</i> s as z. [L. fusio, from fundo, fusum.]<br><br>1. The act or operation of melting or rendering fluid by heat,without the aid of a solvent; as the fusion of ice or of metals.<br><br>2. The state of being melted or dissolved by heat; a state of fluidity or flowing in consequence of heat; as metals in fusion.<br><br>Watery fusion, the melting of certain crystals by heat in their own water of crystallization.',
  },
  'Fuss': {
    'id': 23196,
    'definition': '<b>FUSS</b>, <i>n.</i> [allied perhaps to Gr. to blow or puff.]<br><br>A tumult; a bustle; but the word is vulgar.',
  },
  'Fust': {
    'id': 23197,
    'definition': '<b>FUST</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fustis, a staff.] The shaft of a column.<br><br><b>FUST</b>, <i>n.</i> A strong musty smell.<br><br><b>FUST\'</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To become moldy; to smell ill.',
  },
  'Fusted': {
    'id': 23198,
    'definition': '<b>FUST\'ED</b>, <i>a.</i> Moldy; ill smelling.',
  },
  'Fustet': {
    'id': 23199,
    'definition': '<b>FUS\'TET</b>, <i>n.</i> The wood of the Rhus cotinus, which yields a fine orange color.',
  },
  'Fustian': {
    'id': 23200,
    'definition': '<b>FUS\'TIAN</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A kind of cotton stuff, or stuff of cotton and linen.<br><br>2. An inflated style of writing; a kind of writing in which high sounding words are used, above the dignity of the thoughts or subject; a swelling style; bombast.<br><br>Fustian is thoughts and words ill sorted.<br><br><b>FUS\'TIAN</b>, <i>a.</i> Made of fustian.<br><br>1. In style, swelling above the dignity of the thoughts or subject; too pompous; ridiculously tumid; bombastic.',
  },
  'Fustianist': {
    'id': 23201,
    'definition': '<b>FUS\'TIANIST</b>, <i>n.</i> One who writes bombast.',
  },
  'Fustic': {
    'id': 23202,
    'definition': '<b>FUS\'TIC</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fustis.] The wood of the Morus tinctoria, a tree growing in the West Indies, imported and used in dyeing yellow.',
  },
  'Fustigation': {
    'id': 23203,
    'definition': '<b>FUSTIGA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. fustigatio, from fustigo, to beat with a cudgel, from fustis, a stick or club.] Among the Ancient Romans, a punishment by beating with a stick or club, inflicted on freemen.',
  },
  'Fustiness': {
    'id': 23204,
    'definition': '<b>FUST\'INESS</b>, <i>n.</i> A fusty state or quality; an ill smell from moldiness, or moldiness itself.',
  },
  'Fusty': {
    'id': 23205,
    'definition': '<b>FUST\'Y</b>, <i>a.</i> [See <a href="dict://Fust">Fust</a>.] Moldy; musty; ill smelling; rank; rancid.',
  },
  'Futile': {
    'id': 23206,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'TILE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. futilis, from futio, to pour out; effutio, to prate or babble; Heb. to utter rashly or foolishly.]<br><br>1. Talkative; loquacious; tattling.<br><br>2. Trifling; of no weight or importance; answering no valuable purpose; worthless.<br><br>3. Of no effect.',
  },
  'Futility': {
    'id': 23207,
    'definition': '<b>FUTIL\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> Talkativeness; loquaciousness; loquacity. [In this sense, not now used.]<br><br>1. Triflingness; unimportance; want of weight or effect; as, to expose the futility of arguments.<br><br>2. The quality of producing no valuable effect, or of coming to nothing; as the futility of measures or schemes.',
  },
  'Futilous': {
    'id': 23208,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'TILOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> Worthless; trifling. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Futtock': {
    'id': 23209,
    'definition': '<b>FUT\'TOCK</b>, <i>n.</i> It is more probably corrupted from foot-lock.]<br><br>In a ship, the futtocks are the middle timbers, between the floor and the upper timbers, or the timbers raised over the keel which form the breadth of the ship.',
  },
  'Future': {
    'id': 23210,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'TURE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. futurus.] That is to be or come hereafter; that will exist at any time after the present, indefinitely. The next moment is future to the present.<br><br>1. The future tense, in grammar, is the modification of a verb which expresses a future act or event.<br><br><b>FU\'TURE</b>, <i>n.</i> Time to come; a time subsequent to the present; as, the future shall be as the present; in future; for the future. In such phrases, time or season is implied.',
  },
  'Futurely': {
    'id': 23211,
    'definition': '<b>FU\'TURELY</b>, <i>adv.</i> In time to come. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Futurition': {
    'id': 23212,
    'definition': '<b>FUTURI\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> The state of being to come or exist hereafter.',
  },
  'Futurity': {
    'id': 23213,
    'definition': '<b>FUTU\'RITY</b>, <i>n.</i> Future time; time to come.<br><br>1. Event to come.<br><br>All futurities are naked before the all-seeing eye.<br><br>2. The state of being yet to come, or to come hereafter.',
  },
  'Fuzz': {
    'id': 23214,
    'definition': '<b>FUZZ</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To fly off in minute particles.<br><br><b>FUZZ</b>, <i>n.</i> Fine, light particles; loose, volatile matter.',
  },
  'Fuzzball': {
    'id': 23215,
    'definition': '<b>FUZZ\'BALL</b>, <i>n.</i> A kind of fungus or mushroom, which when pressed bursts and scatters a fine dust.<br><br>1. A puff.',
