  'Handkerchief': {
    'id': 25173,
    'definition': '<b>HAND\'KERCHIEF</b>, <i>n.</i> [hand and kerchief. See <a href="dict://Kerchief">Kerchief</a>.]<br><br>1. A piece of cloth, usually silk or linen, carried about the person for the purpose of cleaning the face or hands, as occasion requires.<br><br>2. A piece of cloth to be worn about the neck, and sometimes called a neckerchief.',
  },
  'Handlanguage': {
    'id': 25174,
    'definition': '<b>HAND\'LANGUAGE</b>, <i>n.</i> The art of conversing by the hands. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Handle': {
    'id': 25175,
    'definition': '<b>HAND\'LE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. manus.]<br><br>1. To touch; to feel with the hand; to use or hold with the hand.<br><br>The bodies we daily handle--hinder the approach of the part of our hands that press them.<br><br>2. To manage; to use; to wield.<br><br>That fellow handles a bow like a crow-keeper.<br><br>3. To make familiar by frequent touching.<br><br>The breeders in Flanders--handle their colts six months every year.<br><br>4. To treat; to discourse on; to discuss; to use or manage in writing or speaking. The author handled the subject with address. The speaker handled the arguments to the best advantage.<br><br>5. To use; to deal with; to practice.<br><br>They that handle the law knew me not. Jer 2.<br><br>6. To treat; to use well or ill.<br><br>How wert thou handled?<br><br>7. To manage; to practice on; to transact with.<br><br>You shall see how I will handle her.<br><br><b>HAND\'LE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. ansa.]<br><br>1. That part of a vessel or instrument which is held in the hand when used, as the haft of a sword,the bail of a kettle, &c.<br><br>2. That of which use is made; the instrument of effecting a purpose.',
  },
  'Handlead': {
    'id': 25176,
    'definition': '<b>HAND\'LEAD</b>, <i>n.</i> A lead for sounding.',
  },
  'Handled': {
    'id': 25177,
    'definition': '<b>HAND\'LED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Touched; treated; managed.',
  },
  'Handless': {
