  'Lick': {
    'id': 31902,
    'definition': '<b>LICK</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. lingo; Gr. See <a href="dict://Like">Like</a> and Sleek.]<br><br>1. To pass or draw the tongue over the surface; as, a dog licks a wound.<br><br>2. To lap; to take in by the tongue; as, a dog or cat licks milk. 1 Ki 21.<br><br>To lick up, to devour; to consume entirely.<br><br>Now shall this company lick up all that are round about us, as an ox licketh up the grass of the field. Num 22.<br><br>To lick the dust, to be slain; to perish in battle.<br><br>His enemies shall lick the dust. Psa 72.<br><br><b>LICK</b>, <i>n.</i> In America, a place where beasts of the forest lick for salt, at salt springs.<br><br><b>LICK</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A blow; a stroke. [Not an elegant word.]<br><br>2. A wash; something rubbed on. [Not in use.]<br><br><b>LICK</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To strike repeatedly for punishment; to flog; to chastise with blows. [Not an elegant word; but probably flog, L. fligo, is from the root of this word.]',
  },
  'Licker': {
    'id': 31903,
    'definition': '<b>LICK\'ER</b>, <i>n.</i> One that licks.',
  },
  'Lickerish': {
    'id': 31904,
    'definition': '<b>LICK\'ERISH</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr. sweet. The sense of watery, smooth, sweet, are allied; likeness is often connected with smoothness in radical sense, and sleek is probably from the root of lick, like.]<br><br>1. Nice in the choice of food; dainty; as a lickerish palate.<br><br>2. Eager; greedy to swallow; eager to taste or enjoy; having a keen relish.<br><br>3. Dainty; tempting the appetite; as lickerish baits.',
  },
