  },
  'Miscall': {
    'id': 34359,
    'definition': '<b>MISCALL\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To call by a wrong name; to name improperly.',
  },
  'Miscalled': {
    'id': 34360,
    'definition': '<b>MISCALL\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Misnamed.',
  },
  'Miscalling': {
    'id': 34361,
    'definition': '<b>MISCALL\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Misnaming.',
  },
  'Miscarriage': {
    'id': 34362,
    'definition': '<b>MISCAR\'RIAGE</b>, <i>n.</i> Unfortunate event of an undertaking; failure.<br><br>When a counselor, to save himself,<br><br>Would lay miscarriages upon his prince.<br><br>1. Ill conduct; evil or improper behavior; as the failings and miscarriages of the righteous.<br><br>2. Abortion; the act of bringing forth before the time.',
  },
  'Miscarry': {
    'id': 34363,
    'definition': '<b>MISCAR\'RY</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To fail of the intended effect; not to succeed; to be unsuccessful; to suffer defeat; applied to persons or undertakings, and to things. We say, a project, scheme, design, enterprise, attempt, has miscarried.<br><br>Have you not heart of Frederick, the great soldier, who miscarried at sea?<br><br>My ships have all miscarried.<br><br>1. To bring forth young before the proper time; to suffer abortion.',
  },
  'Miscarrying': {
    'id': 34364,
    'definition': '<br>MISCAR\'RYING	ppr. Failing of the intended effect; suffering abortion. Hosea 9.',
  },
  'Miscast': {
    'id': 34365,
    'definition': '<b>MISC`AST</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To cast or reckon erroneously.<br><br><b>MISC`AST</b>, <i>pp.</i> Erroneously cast or reckoned.<br><br><b>MISC`AST</b>, <i>n.</i> An erroneous cast or reckoning.',
  },
  'Miscasting': {
    'id': 34366,
    'definition': '<b>MISC`ASTING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Casting or reckoning erroneously.',
  },
  'Miscellanarian': {
    'id': 34367,
    'definition': '<b>MISCELLANA\'RIAN</b>, <i>a.</i> [See <a href="dict://Miscellany">Miscellany</a>.]<br><br>Belonging to miscellanies; of miscellanies.<br><br>Miscellanarian authors.<br><br><b>MISCELLANA\'RIAN</b>, <i>n.</i> A writer of miscellanies.',
  },
  'Miscellane': {
    'id': 34368,
    'definition': '<b>MIS\'CELLANE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. miscellaneus.] A mixture of two or more sorts of grain; now called meslin.',
  },
  'Miscellaneous': {
    'id': 34369,
    'definition': '<b>MISCELLA\'NEOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. miscellaneus, from misceo, to mix.]<br><br>Mixed; mingled; consisting of several kinds; as a miscellaneous publication; a miscellaneous rabble.',
  },
  'Miscellaneousness': {
    'id': 34370,
    'definition': '<b>MISCELLA\'NEOUSNESS</b>, <i>n.</i> The state of being mixed; composition of various kinds.',
  },
  'Miscellany': {
    'id': 34371,
    'definition': '<b>MIS\'CELLANY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L.miscellanea, from misceo, to mix.]<br><br>1. A mass or mixture of various kinds; particularly,<br><br>2. A book or pamphlet containing a collection of compositions on various subjects, or a collection of various kinds of compositions.<br><br><b>MIS\'CELLANY</b>, <i>a.</i> Miscellaneous.',
  },
  'Miscenter': {
    'id': 34372,
    'definition': '<b>MISCEN\'TER</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To place amiss. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Mischance': {
    'id': 34373,
    'definition': '<b>MISCH`ANCE</b>, <i>n.</i> Ill luck; ill fortune; misfortune; mishap; misadventure.<br><br>It is a man\'s unhappiness, his mischance or calamity, but not his fault.',
  },
  'Mischaracterize': {
    'id': 34374,
    'definition': '<b>MISCHAR\'ACTERIZE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [See <a href="dict://Character">Character</a>.] To characterize falsely or erroneously; to give a wrong character to.<br><br>They totally mischaracterize the action.',
  },
  'Mischarge': {
    'id': 34375,
    'definition': '<b>MISCH`ARGE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To mistake in charging, as an account.<br><br><b>MISCH`ARGE</b>, <i>n.</i> A mistake in charging, as an account; an erroneous entry in an account.',
  },
  'Mischief': {
    'id': 34376,
    'definition': '<b>MIS\'CHIEF</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. Harm; hurt; injury; damage; evil, whether intended or not. A new law is made to remedy the mischief.<br><br>2. Intentional injury; harm or damage done by design.<br><br>Thy tongue deviseth mischief. Psa 52.<br><br>3. Ill consequence; evil; vexatious affair.<br><br>The mischief was, these allies would never allow that the common enemy was subdued.<br><br><b>MIS\'CHIEF</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To hurt; to harm; to injure.',
  },
  'Mischief-Maker': {
    'id': 34377,
    'definition': '<b>MIS\'CHIEF-MAKER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who makes mischief; one who excites or instigates quarrels or enmity.',
  },
  'Mischief-Making': {
    'id': 34378,
    'definition': '<b>MIS\'CHIEF-MAKING</b>, <i>a.</i> Causing harm; exciting enmity or quarrels.',
  },
  'Mischievous': {
    'id': 34379,
    'definition': '<b>MIS\'CHIEVOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> Harmful; hurtful; injurious; making mischief; of persons; as a mischievous man or disposition.<br><br>1. Hurtful; noxious; as a mischievous thing.<br><br>2. Inclined to do harm; as a mischievous boy.',
  },
  'Mischievously': {
    'id': 34380,
    'definition': '<b>MIS\'CHIEVOUSLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With injury, hurt, loss or damage. We say, the law operates mischievously.<br><br>1. With evil intention or disposition. The injury was done mischievously.',
  },
  'Mischievousness': {
    'id': 34381,
    'definition': '<b>MIS\'CHIEVOUSNESS</b>, <i>n.</i> Hurtfulness; noxiousness.<br><br>1. Disposition to do harm, or to vex or annoy; as the mischievousness of youth.<br><br>Mischief denotes injury, harm or damage of less malignity and magnitude than what are usually called crimes. We never give the name of mischief to theft, robbery or murder. And it so commonly implies intention in committing petty offenses, that it shocks us to hear the word applied to the calamities inflicted by Providence. We say, a tempest has done great damage,but not mischief. In like manner, the adjective mischievous is not applied to thieves, pirates and other felons,but to persons committing petty trespasses and offenses.',
  },
  'Mischna': {
    'id': 34382,
