  'Ogham': {
    'id': 36922,
    'definition': '<b>O\'GHAM</b>, <i>n.</i> A particular kind of stenography or writing in cipher practiced by the Irish.',
  },
  'Ogive': {
    'id': 36923,
    'definition': '<b>OGIVE</b>, <i>n.</i> o\'jiv. In architecture, an arch or branch of the Gothic vault, which passing diagonally from one angle to another forms a cross with the other arches. The middle where the ogives cross each other, is called the key. The members or moldings of the ogives are called nerves, branches or reins, and the arches which separate the ogives, double arches.',
  },
  'Ogle': {
    'id': 36924,
    'definition': '<b>O\'GLE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. oculus. See <a href="dict://Eye">Eye</a>.]<br><br>To view with side glances, as in fondness or with design to attract notice.<br><br>And ogling all their audience, then they speak.<br><br><b>O\'GLE</b>, <i>n.</i> A side glance or look.',
  },
  'Ogler': {
    'id': 36925,
    'definition': '<b>O\'GLER</b>, <i>n.</i> One that ogles.',
  },
  'Ogling': {
    'id': 36926,
    'definition': '<b>O\'GLING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Viewing with side glances.<br><br><b>O\'GLING</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of viewing with side glances.',
  },
  'Oglio': {
    'id': 36927,
    'definition': '<b>OGLIO</b>, <i>now</i> written olio, which see.',
  },
  'Ogre': {
    'id': 36928,
    'definition': '<b>O\'GRE,<br><br>O\'GRESS</b>, <i>n.</i> An imaginary monster of the East.<br><br><b>O\'GRESS</b>, <i>n.</i> In heraldry, a cannon ball of a black color.',
  },
  'Oh': {
    'id': 36929,
    'definition': '<b>OH</b>, <i>exclam.</i> Denoting surprise, pain, sorrow or anxiety.',
  },
  'Oil': {
    'id': 36930,
    'definition': '<b>OIL</b>, <i>n.</i> It seems to be named from its inflammability, for aelan, is to kindle, and to oil; hence anaelan, to anneal; aeled, fire. L. oleum; Gr.]<br><br>An unctuous substance expressed or drawn from several animal and vegetable substances. The distinctive characters of oil are inflammability, fluidity, and insolubility in water. Oils are fixed or fat, and volatile or essential. They have a smooth feel, and most of them have little taste or smell. Animal oil is found in all animal substances. Vegetable oils are produced by expression, infusion or distillation.<br><br><b>OIL</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To smear or rub over with oil; to lubricate with oil; to anoint with oil.<br><br><b>OIL\'-BAG</b>, <i>n.</i> A bag, cyst or gland in animals containing oil.<br><br><b>OIL\'-COLOR</b>, <i>n.</i> A color made by grinding a coloring substance in oil.',
  },
  'Oil-Nut': {
    'id': 36931,
    'definition': '<b>OIL\'-NUT</b>, <i>n.</i> The butternut of North America.<br><br><b>OIL\'-NUT,<br><br>OIL\'-TREE</b>, <i>n.</i> A plant, a species of Ricinus, the palma Christi, or castor, from which is procured castor oil.<br><br><b>OIL\'-SHOP</b>, <i>n.</i> A shop where oils and pickles are sold.',
  },
  'Oiled': {
    'id': 36932,
    'definition': '<b>OIL\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Smeared or anointed with oil.',
  },
  'Oiler': {
    'id': 36933,
    'definition': '<b>OIL\'ER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who deals in oils and pickles.<br><br><b>OIL\'-GAS</b>, <i>n.</i> Inflammable gas procured from oil, and used for lighting streets and apartments in buildings.',
  },
  'Oiliness': {
    'id': 36934,
    'definition': '<b>OIL\'INESS</b>, <i>n.</i> The quality of being oily; unctuousness; greasiness; a quality approaching that of oil.',
  },
  'Oiling': {
    'id': 36935,
    'definition': '<b>OIL\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Smearing or anointing with oil.',
  },
  'Oilman': {
    'id': 36936,
    'definition': '<b>OIL\'MAN</b>, <i>n.</i> One who deals in oils and pickles.',
  },
  'Oily': {
    'id': 36937,
    'definition': '<b>OIL\'Y</b>, <i>a.</i><br><br>1. Consisting of oil; containing oil; having the qualities of oil; as oily matter or substance.<br><br>2. Resembling oil; as an oily appearance.<br><br>3. Fatty; greasy.',
  },
  'Oily-Grain': {
    'id': 36938,
    'definition': '<b>OILY-GRAIN</b>, <i>n.</i> A plant.',
  },
  'Oily-Palm': {
    'id': 36939,
    'definition': '<b>OILY-PALM</b>, <i>n.</i> A tree.',
  },
  'Oint': {
    'id': 36940,
    'definition': '<b>OINT</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. ungo, like joindre from jungo.]<br><br>To anoint; to smear with an unctuous substance.<br><br>They oint their naked limbs with mother\'d oil.',
  },
  'Ointed': {
    'id': 36941,
    'definition': '<b>OINT\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Anointed; smeared with an oily or greasy matter.',
  },
  'Ointing': {
    'id': 36942,
    'definition': '<b>OINT\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Anointing.',
  },
  'Ointment': {
    'id': 36943,
    'definition': '<b>OINT\'MENT</b>, <i>n.</i> Unguent; any soft, unctuous substance or compound, used for smearing, particularly the body or a diseased part.',
  },
  'Oisanite': {
    'id': 36944,
    'definition': '<b>OIS\'ANITE</b>, <i>n.</i> Pyramidical ore of titanium.',
  },
  'Oke': {
    'id': 36945,
    'definition': '<b>OKE</b>, <i>n.</i> An Egyptian and Turkish weight, equal to about two pounds and three quarters, English avoirdupois weight.',
  },
  'Oker': {
    'id': 36946,
    'definition': '<b>OKER</b>. [See <a href="dict://Ocher">Ocher</a>.]',
  },
  'Olate': {
    'id': 36947,
    'definition': '<b>OLATE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. To lay waste, alone.]<br><br>1. To deprive of inhabitants; to make desert. The earth was nearly desolated by the flood.<br><br>2. To lay waste; to ruin; to ravage; to destroy improvements or works of art. An inundation desolates fields. Whole countries have been desolated by armies.',
  },
  'Old': {
    'id': 36948,
    'definition': '<b>OLD</b>, <i>a.<br><br></i> 1. Advanced far in years or life; having lived beyond the middle period, or rather towards the end of life, or towards the end of the ordinary term of living; applied to animals or plants; as an old man; an old age; an old camel or horse; an old tree. This adjective is placed after the noun that designates the time lived.<br><br>Abraham was seventy five years old when he departed from Haran. Gen 12.<br><br>2. Having been long made or used; decayed by time; as an old garment; an old house.<br><br>3. Being of long continuance; begun long ago; as an old acquaintance.<br><br>4. Having been long made; not new or fresh; as old wine.<br><br>5. Being of a former year\'s growth; not of the last crop; as old wheat; old hay.<br><br>6. Ancient; that existed in former ages; as the old inhabitants of Britain; the old Romans.<br><br>7. Of any duration whatever; as a year old; seven years old. How old art thou?<br><br>8. subsisting before something else. He built a new house on the site of the old one. The old law is repealed by the new.<br><br>9. Long practiced. he is grown old in vice. He is an old offender.<br><br>10. That has been long cultivated; as old land; an old farm; opposed to new land, land lately cleared and cultivated.<br><br>11. More than enough; great.<br><br>If a man were porter of hellgate, he should have old turning the key.<br><br>12. In vulgar language, crafty; cunning.<br><br>Of old, long ago; from ancient times; as in days of old.<br><br>We apply old chiefly to things subject to decay. We never say, the old sun, or an old mountain.',
  },
  'Old-Fashioned': {
    'id': 36949,
    'definition': '<b>OLD-FASH\'IONED</b>, <i>a.</i> formed according to obsolete fashion or custom; as an old-fashioned dress.<br><br>Old-fashioned men of wit.',
  },
  'Old-Wife': {
    'id': 36950,
    'definition': '<b>OLD-WIFE</b>, <i>n.</i><br><br>1. A contemptuous name for an old prating woman. 1 Tim 4.<br><br>2. A fish of the genus Labrus, and another of the genus Balistes.',
  },
  'Olden': {
    'id': 36951,
    'definition': '<b>OLDEN</b>, <i>a.</i> Old; ancient. [Used in poetry.]',
  },
  'Oldness': {
    'id': 36952,
    'definition': '<b>OLDNESS</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. Old age; an advanced state of life or existence; as the oldness of a man, of an elephant or a tree.<br><br>2. The state of being old, or of a long continuance; as the oldness of a building or a garment.<br><br>3. Antiquity; as the oldness of monuments.',
  },
  'Oleaginous': {
    'id': 36953,
    'definition': '<b>OLEAG\'INOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. oleaginus, from oleum, oil.] Having the qualities of oil; oily; unctuous.',
  },
  'Oleaginousness': {
    'id': 36954,
    'definition': '<b>OLEAG\'INOUSNESS</b>, <i>n.</i> Oiliness.',
  },
  'Oleander': {
    'id': 36955,
    'definition': '<b>OLEAN\'DER</b>, <i>n.</i> a plant of the genus Nerium the rose-bay or South sea rose; a beautiful shrub with flowers in clusters, of a fine purple color, but of an indifferent smell. The plant, especially the bark of the roots, is said to be poisonous.',
  },
  'Oleaster': {
    'id': 36956,
    'definition': '<b>OLEAS\'TER</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. from olea, the olive tree.]<br><br>A plant of the genus Elaeagnus; the wild olive.',
  },
  'Oleate': {
    'id': 36957,
    'definition': '<b>O\'LEATE</b>, <i>n.</i> A compound of oleic acid with a salifiable base.',
  },
  'Olefiant': {
    'id': 36958,
    'definition': '<b>OLEF\'IANT</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. oleo, olfacio.] Olefiant gas is a compound of one prime of carbon and one of hydrogen, called by Ure carbureted hydrogen, to distinguish it from the gas resulting from one prime of carbon and two of hydrogen, which he calls subcarbureted hydrogen.<br><br>Olefiant gas, is so called from its property of forming with chlorin a compound resembling oil.',
  },
  'Oleic': {
    'id': 36959,
    'definition': '<b>O\'LEIC</b>, <i>a.</i> [from oil.] The oleic acid is obtained from a soap made by digesting hog\'s lard in potash lye.',
  },
  'Oleosaccharum': {
    'id': 36960,
    'definition': '<b>OLEOSAC\'CHARUM</b>, <i>n.</i> A mixture of oil and sugar.',
  },
  'Oleose': {
    'id': 36961,
    'definition': '<b>O\'LEOSE,<br><br>O\'LEOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. olcosus.] Oily. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Oleraceous': {
    'id': 36962,
    'definition': '<b>OLERA\'CEOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. oleracceus, from olus, oleris, pot-herbs.]<br><br>Pertaining to pot-herbs; of the nature or qualities of herbs for cookery.',
  },
  'Olfact': {
    'id': 36963,
    'definition': '<b>OLFACT\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. olfacto, olfacio; oleo, to smell, and facio, to make.]<br><br>To smell; used in burlesque, but not otherwise authorized.',
  },
  'Olfactory': {
    'id': 36964,
    'definition': '<b>OLFACT\'ORY</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. olfacio, supra.] Pertaining to smelling; having the sense of smelling; as olfactory nerves.',
  },
  'Oliban': {
    'id': 36965,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'IBAN,<br><br>OLIBA\'NUM</b>, <i>n.</i> [The word signifies then frankincense, and it is so named from its whiteness.]<br><br>A gum-resin consisting of tears or drops, of a yellow transparent color and disagreeable smell. It is brought from Turkey and the East Indies. It is not, as Linne supposed, produced by the Juniperus Lycia, but from a different tree growing in Arabia and Hindoostan. In Arabia, luban is applied to benzoin, which is generally used for incense, and oliban is called condur, whence Gr. In medicine, it is used in fumigations as a resolvent.<br><br>Thompson says olibanum is produced by different trees and in different countries.',
  },
  'Olid': {
    'id': 36966,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'ID,<br><br>OL\'IDOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. olidus, from oleo, to smell.] Fetid; having a strong disagreeable smell. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Oligarchal': {
    'id': 36967,
    'definition': '<b>OLIGARCH\'AL,<br><br>OLIGARCH\'ICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [See <a href="dict://Oligrachy">Oligrachy</a>.] Pertaining to oligrachy, or government by a few.',
  },
  'Oligist': {
    'id': 36968,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'IGIST,\'IC</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr. least.] Oligist iron, so called, is a crystallized tritoxyd of iron.',
  },
  'Oligrachy': {
    'id': 36969,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'IGRACHY</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. few, and rule.]<br><br>A form of government in which the supreme power is placed in a few hands; a species of aristocracy.',
  },
  'Olio': {
    'id': 36970,
    'definition': '<b>O\'LIO</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. olla, a pot.]<br><br>1. A mixture; a medley.<br><br>2. A miscellany; a collection of various pieces; applied to musical collections.',
  },
  'Olitory': {
    'id': 36971,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'ITORY</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. olitor, a gardener, from olus, pot-herbs.]<br><br>Belonging to a kitchen garden; as olitory seeds. It may perhaps be used as a noun.',
  },
  'Olivaceous': {
    'id': 36972,
    'definition': '<b>OLIVA\'CEOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [from L. oliva, olive.] Of the color of the olive.',
  },
  'Olivaster': {
    'id': 36973,
    'definition': '<b>OLIVAS\'TER</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. oliva, olive.] Of the color of the olive, tawny.',
  },
  'Olive': {
    'id': 36974,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'IVE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. oliva, from olea, an olive tree; Gr. See <a href="dict://Oil]<br><br>">Oil]<br><br></a> A plant or tree of the genus Olea. The common olive tree grows in warm climates and rises to the height of twenty or thirty feet, having an upright stem with numerous branches. This tree is much cultivated in the south of Europe for its fruit, from which is expressed the olive oil, and which is used also for pickles.',
  },
  'Olive-Yard': {
    'id': 36975,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'IVE-YARD</b>, <i>n.</i> An inclosure or piece of ground in which olives are cultivated. Exo 23.',
  },
  'Olived': {
    'id': 36976,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'IVED</b>, <i>a.</i> Decorated with olive trees.',
  },
  'Olivenite': {
    'id': 36977,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'IVENITE</b>, <i>n.</i> An ore of copper.',
  },
  'Olivin': {
    'id': 36978,
    'definition': '<b>OL\'IVIN,<br><br>OL\'IVINE</b>, <i>n.</i> [from olive.] A subspecies of prismatic chrysolite of a brownish green, often inclining to a yellowish or grayish green, usually found in roundish grains in other stones; sometimes in large masses, but not crystallized. It is a constituent of many lavas and frequently occurs in basaltic rocks.',
  },
  'Olympean': {
    'id': 36979,
    'definition': '<b>OLYM\'PEAN</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to Olympus; or to Olympis, a town in Greece.<br><br>Olympic games, or Olympics, solemn games among the ancient Greeks, dedicated to Olympian Jupiter, and celebrated once in four years at Olympia. [See <a href="dict://Olympiad">Olympiad</a>.]',
  },
  'Olympiad': {
    'id': 36980,
    'definition': '<b>OLYM\'PIAD</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. Olympias; Gr. from Olympus, a mountain of Macedonia.]<br><br>A period of four years reckoned from one celebration of the Olympic games to another, and constituting an important epoch in history and chronology. The first Olympiad commenced 775 years before the birth of Christ, and 22 years before the foundation of Rome. The computation by Olympiads ceased at the three hundred and sixty fourth Olympiad, in the year 440 of the christian era.',
  },
  'Omber': {
    'id': 36981,
    'definition': '<b>OM\'BER,<br><br>OM\'BRE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. homo.]<br><br>A game at cards, borrowed from the Spaniards, usually played by three persons, though sometimes by two or five.',
  },
  'Ombrometer': {
    'id': 36982,
    'definition': '<b>OMBROM\'ETER</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. rain, and measure.]<br><br>A machine or instrument to measure the quantity of rain that falls.',
  },
  'Omega': {
    'id': 36983,
    'definition': '<b>OME\'GA</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. great O.] The name of the last letter of the Greek alphabet, as Alpha, A, is the first. Hence in Scripture, Alpha and Omega denotes the first and last, the beginning and the ending. Rev.',
  },
  'Omelet': {
    'id': 36984,
    'definition': '<b>OM\'ELET</b>, <i>n.</i> A kind of pancake or fritter made with eggs and other ingredients.',
  },
  'Omen': {
    'id': 36985,
    'definition': '<b>O\'MEN</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. omen; Heb. an augur.]<br><br>A sign or indication of some future event; a prognostic. Superstition and ignorance multiply omens; philosophy and truth reject all omens, except such as may be called causes of the events. Without a miracle, how can one event be the omen of another with which it has no connection?',
  },
  'Omened': {
    'id': 36986,
    'definition': '<b>O\'MENED</b>, <i>a.</i> Containing an omen or prognostic.',
  },
  'Omentum': {
    'id': 36987,
    'definition': '<b>OMENT\'UM</b>, <i>n.</i> [L.] In anatomy, the caul or epiploon; a membranaceous covering of the bowels, being placed under the peritoneum and immediately above the intestines.<br><br><b>O\'MER</b>, <i>n.</i> [Heb.] A Hebrew measure containing ten baths, or seventy five gallons and five pints of liquids, and eight bushels of things dry. It was the largest measure used by the Jews. It is written also homer and chomer. This word is used by the prophets, but the corresponding measure is called by the historical writers corus.',
  },
  'Omer': {
    'id': 36988,
    'definition': '<b>OMER<br><br>OM\'INATE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. ominor, from omen.] To presage; to foreshow; to foretoken. [Little used.]<br><br><b>OM\'INATE</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To foretoken.',
  },
  'Omination': {
    'id': 36989,
    'definition': '<b>OMINA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> A foreboding; a presaging; prognostic. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Ominous': {
    'id': 36990,
    'definition': '<b>OM\'INOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. ominosus.]<br><br>1. Foreboding or presaging evil; indicating a future evil event; inauspicious.<br><br>In the heathen worship of God, a sacrifice without a heart was accounted ominous.<br><br>2. Foreshowing or exhibiting signs of good.<br><br>Though he had a good ominous name to have made peace, nothing followed.',
  },
  'Ominously': {
    'id': 36991,
    'definition': '<b>OM\'INOUSLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With good or bad omens.',
  },
  'Ominousness': {
    'id': 36992,
    'definition': '<b>OM\'INOUSNESS</b>, <i>n.</i> The quality of being ominous.',
  },
  'Omissible': {
    'id': 36993,
    'definition': '<b>OMIS\'SIBLE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. omissus. See <a href="dict://Omit">Omit</a>.] That may be omitted.',
  },
  'Omission': {
    'id': 36994,
    'definition': '<b>OMIS\'SION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. omissio, from omitto, omissus.]<br><br>1. Neglect or failure to do something which a person had power to do, or which duty required to be done. Omission may be innocent or criminal; innocent, when no duty demands performance, but criminal when duty is neglected.<br><br>The most natural division of all offenses, is into those of omission and those of commission.<br><br>2. A leaving out; neglect or failure; to insert or mention; as the omission of a word or clause.',
  },
  'Omissive': {
    'id': 36995,
    'definition': '<b>OMIS\'SIVE</b>, <i>a.</i> Leaving out.',
  },
  'Omit': {
    'id': 36996,
    'definition': '<b>OMIT\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. omitto; ob and mitto, to send.]<br><br>1. To leave, pass by or neglect; to fail or forbear to do or to use; as, to omit an opportunity of writing a letter. To omit known duty is criminal.<br><br>2. To leave out; not to insert or mention; as, to omit an important word in a deed; to omit invidious comparisons; to omit a passage in reading or transcribing.',
  },
  'Omittance': {
    'id': 36997,
    'definition': '<b>OMIT\'TANCE</b>, <i>n.</i> Forbearance; neglect. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Omitted': {
    'id': 36998,
    'definition': '<b>OMIT\'TED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Neglected; passed by; left out.',
  },
  'Omitting': {
    'id': 36999,
    'definition': '<b>OMIT\'TING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Neglecting or failing to do or use; passing by; leaving out.',
  },
  'Omnifarious': {
    'id': 37000,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIFA\'RIOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [Low L. omnifarius.] Of all varieties, forms or kinds.',
  },
  'Omniferous': {
    'id': 37001,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIF\'EROUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. omnifer; omnis, all, and fero, to bear.]<br><br>All-bearing; producing all kinds.',
  },
  'Omnific': {
    'id': 37002,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIF\'IC</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. omnis, all, and facio, to make.] All-creating.<br><br>Thou deep, peace! said then th\' omnific word, your discord end.',
  },
  'Omniform': {
    'id': 37003,
    'definition': '<b>OM\'NIFORM</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. omnis, all, and forma, form.] Having every form or shape.',
  },
  'Omniformity': {
    'id': 37004,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIFORM\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> The quality of having every form.',
  },
  'Omnigenous': {
    'id': 37005,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIG\'ENOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. omnigenus; omnis, all, every, and genus, kind.] Consisting of all kinds.',
  },
  'Omniparity': {
    'id': 37006,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIPAR\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. omnis, all, and par, equal.] General equality.',
  },
  'Omnipercipience': {
    'id': 37007,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIPERCIP\'IENCE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. omnis, and percipiens, perceiving.]<br><br>Perception of every thing.',
  },
  'Omnipercipient': {
    'id': 37008,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIPERCIP\'IENT</b>, <i>a.</i> Perceiving every thing.',
  },
  'Omnipotence': {
    'id': 37009,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIP\'OTENCE,\'OTENCY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. omnipotens; omnis, all, and potens, powerful.]',
  },
  'Omnipotent': {
    'id': 37010,
    'definition': '<br>OMNIP\'OTENT, a. [supra.]<br><br>1. Almighty; possessing unlimited power; all powerful. The being that can create worlds must be omnipotent.<br><br>2. Having unlimited power of a particular kind; as omnipotent love.',
  },
  'Omnipotently': {
    'id': 37011,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIP\'OTENTLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With almighty power.',
  },
  'Omnipresence': {
    'id': 37012,
    'definition': '<b>OMNIPRES\'ENCE</b>, <i>n.</i> s as z. [L. omnis, and presens, present.]<br><br>Presence in every place at the same time; unbounded or universal presence; ubiquity. Omnipresence is an attribute peculiar to God.',
  },
