    'definition': '<b>OUT\'LAY</b>, <i>n.</i> A laying out or expending; expenditure.',
  },
  'Outleap': {
    'id': 37552,
    'definition': '<b>OUTLE\'AP</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To leap beyond; to pass by leaping.<br><br><b>OUT\'LEAP</b>, <i>n.</i> Sally; flight; escape.',
  },
  'Outlet': {
    'id': 37553,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'LET</b>, <i>n.</i> Passage outward; the place or the means by which any thing escapes or is discharged. A gate is the outlet of a city or fort. The mouth of a river is its outlet. Colonies are the outlets of a populous nation.',
  },
  'Outlicker': {
    'id': 37554,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'LICKER</b>, <i>n.</i> In ships, a small piece of timber fastened to the top of the poop.',
  },
  'Outlie': {
    'id': 37555,
    'definition': '<b>OUTLI\'E</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in lying.',
  },
  'Outlier': {
    'id': 37556,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'LIER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who does not reside in the place with which his office or duty connects him.',
  },
  'Outline': {
    'id': 37557,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'LINE</b>, <i>n.</i> Contour; the line by which a figure is defined; the exterior line.<br><br><b>OUT\'LINE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To draw the exterior line; to delineate; to sketch.',
  },
  'Outlive': {
    'id': 37558,
    'definition': '<b>OUTLIVE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outliv\'.<br><br>1. To live beyond; to survive; to live after something has ceased; as, a man may outlive his children; a person may outlive his estate, his fame and his usefulness.<br><br>They live too long who happiness outlive.<br><br>2. To live better or to better purpose.',
  },
  'Outliver': {
    'id': 37559,
    'definition': '<b>OUTLIV\'ER</b>, <i>n.</i> A survivor.',
  },
  'Outlook': {
    'id': 37560,
    'definition': '<b>OUTLOOK\'</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To face down; to browbeat.<br><br>2. To select. [Not in use.]<br><br><b>OUT\'LOOK</b>, <i>n.</i> Vigilant watch; foresight. [But look-out is generally used.]',
  },
  'Outlope': {
    'id': 37561,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'LOPE</b>, <i>n.</i> [See <a href="dict://Lope">Lope</a> and Leap.] An excursion. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Outluster': {
    'id': 37562,
    'definition': '<b>OUTLUS\'TER,<br><br>OUTLUS\'TRE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To excel in brightness.',
  },
  'Outlying': {
    'id': 37563,
    'definition': '<b>OUTLY\'ING</b>, <i>a.<br><br></i> 1. Lying or being at a distance from the main body or design.<br><br>2. Being on the exterior or frontier.',
  },
  'Outmarch': {
    'id': 37564,
    'definition': '<b>OUTM\'ARCH</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To march faster than; to march so as to leave behind.<br><br>The horse outmarched the foot.',
  },
  'Outmeasure': {
    'id': 37565,
    'definition': '<b>OUTMEASURE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outmezh\'ur. To exceed in measure or extent.',
  },
  'Outmost': {
    'id': 37566,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'MOST</b>, <i>a.</i> Farthest outward; most remote from the middle.',
  },
  'Outnumber': {
    'id': 37567,
    'definition': '<b>OUTNUM\'BER</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in number. The troops outnumbered those of the enemy.',
  },
  'Outpace': {
    'id': 37568,
    'definition': '<b>OUTPA\'CE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To outgo; to leave behind.',
  },
  'Outparamour': {
    'id': 37569,
    'definition': '<b>OUTPAR\'AMOUR</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [See <a href="dict://Paramour">Paramour</a>.] To exceed in keeping mistresses.',
  },
  'Outparish': {
    'id': 37570,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'PARISH</b>, <i>n.</i> A parish lying without the walls, or on the border.',
  },
  'Outpart': {
    'id': 37571,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'PART</b>, <i>n.</i> A part remote from the center or main part.',
  },
  'Outpass': {
    'id': 37572,
    'definition': '<b>OUTP\'ASS</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To pass beyond; to exceed in progress.',
  },
  'Outpoise': {
    'id': 37573,
    'definition': '<b>OUTPOISE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outpoiz\'. To outweigh.',
  },
  'Outporch': {
    'id': 37574,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'PORCH</b>, <i>n.</i> An entrance.',
  },
  'Outpost': {
    'id': 37575,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'POST</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A post or station without the limits of a camp, or at a distance from the main body of an army.<br><br>2. The troops placed at such a station.',
  },
  'Outpour': {
    'id': 37576,
    'definition': '<b>OUTPOUR</b>, <i>v.t.<br>1.</i> To pour out; to send forth in a stream.<br><br>2. To effuse.',
  },
  'Outpouring': {
    'id': 37577,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'POURING</b>, <i>n.</i> A pouring out; effusion.',
  },
  'Outpray': {
    'id': 37578,
    'definition': '<b>OUTPRA\'Y</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in prayer or in earnestness of entreaty.',
  },
  'Outpreach': {
    'id': 37579,
    'definition': '<b>OUTPRE\'ACH</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To surpass in preaching; to produce more effect in inculcating lessons or truth.<br><br>And for a villain\'s quick conversion a pill\'ry can outpreach a parson.',
  },
  'Outprize': {
    'id': 37580,
    'definition': '<b>OUTPRI\'ZE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in value or estimated worth.',
  },
  'Outrage': {
    'id': 37581,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'RAGE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. ultra, beyond.]<br><br>To treat with violence and wrong; to abuse by rude or insolent language; to injure by rough, rude treatment of any kind.<br><br>Base and insolent minds outrage men, when they have hopes of doing it without a return.<br><br>This interview outrages all decency.<br><br><b>OUT\'RAGE</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To commit exorbitances; to be guilty of violent rudeness.<br><br><b>OUT\'RAGE</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> Injurious violence offered to persons or things; excessive abuse; wanton mischief. Rude abusive language, scurrility, or opprobrious and contemptuous words, may be an outrage to persons, or to decency and civility. A violent attack upon person or property is an outrage.<br><br>He wrought great outrages, wasting all the country where he went.',
  },
  'Outrageous': {
    'id': 37582,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRA\'GEOUS</b>, <i>a.<br><br></i> 1. Violent; furious; exorbitant; exceeding all bounds of moderation; as outrageous villainies; outrageous talk; outrageous abuse.<br><br>2. Excessive; exceeding reason or decency; as outrageous panegyric.<br><br>3. Enormous; atrocious; as outrageous crimes.<br><br>4. Tumultuous; turbulent.',
  },
  'Outrageously': {
    'id': 37583,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRA\'GEOUSLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With great violence; furiously; excessively.',
  },
  'Outrageousness': {
    'id': 37584,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRA\'GEOUSNESS</b>, <i>n.</i> Fury; violence; enormity.',
  },
  'Outraze': {
    'id': 37585,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRA\'ZE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To raze to extermination.',
  },
  'Outre': {
    'id': 37586,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRE</b>, <i>a.</i> ootra\'y. Being out of the common course or limits; extravagant.',
  },
  'Outreach': {
    'id': 37587,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRE\'ACH</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To go or extend beyond.',
  },
  'Outreason': {
    'id': 37588,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRE\'ASON</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To excel or surpass in reasoning.',
  },
  'Outreckon': {
    'id': 37589,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRECK\'ON</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in assumed computation.',
  },
  'Outreign': {
    'id': 37590,
    'definition': '<b>OUTREIGN</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To reign through the whole of.',
  },
  'Outride': {
    'id': 37591,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRI\'DE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To pass by riding; to ride faster than.<br><br><b>OUTRI\'DE</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To travel about on horseback, or in a vehicle.',
  },
  'Outrider': {
    'id': 37592,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'RIDER</b>, <i>n.</i><br><br>1. A summoner whose office is to cite men before the sheriff. [Not used.]<br><br>2. One who travels about on horseback.<br><br>3. An attending servant.',
  },
  'Outrigger': {
    'id': 37593,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'RIGGER</b>, <i>n.</i> In seamen\'s language, a strong beam fixed on the side of a ship and projecting from it, in order to secure the masts in the operation of careening, by counteracting the strain it suffers from the effort of the careening tackle; also, a boom occasionally used in the tops to thrust out the breast back-stays to windward, to increase the angle of tension, and give additional security to the topmast. [See <a href="dict://Prow">Prow</a>.]',
  },
  'Outright': {
    'id': 37594,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'RIGHT</b>, <i>adv.</i><br><br>1. Immediately; without delay; at once.<br><br>2. Completely.',
  },
  'Outrival': {
    'id': 37595,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRI\'VAL</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To surpass in excellence.',
  },
  'Outroar': {
    'id': 37596,
    'definition': '<b>OUTROAR</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in roaring.',
  },
  'Outrode': {
    'id': 37597,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'RODE</b>, <i>n.</i> An excursion.',
  },
  'Outroot': {
    'id': 37598,
    'definition': '<b>OUTROOT\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To eradicate; to extirpate.',
  },
  'Outrun': {
    'id': 37599,
    'definition': '<b>OUTRUN\'</b>, <i>v.t<br><br></i> 1. To exceed in running; to leave behind in running.<br><br>2. To exceed; as, to outrun one\'s income.',
  },
  'Outsail': {
    'id': 37600,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSA\'IL</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To sail faster than; to leave behind in sailing.',
  },
  'Outscape': {
    'id': 37601,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSCA\'PE</b>, <i>n.</i> Power of escaping. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Outscorn': {
    'id': 37602,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSCORN\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To bear down or confront by contempt; to despise.',
  },
  'Outscourings': {
    'id': 37603,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSCOUR\'INGS</b>, <i>n.</i> [out and scour.] Substances washed or scoured out.',
  },
  'Outsell': {
    'id': 37604,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSELL\'</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To exceed in amount of sales.<br><br>2. To exceed in the prices of things sold.<br><br>3. To gain a higher price.',
  },
  'Outset': {
    'id': 37605,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'SET</b>, <i>n.</i> Beginning; first entrance on any business.<br><br>Every thing almost depends upon giving a proper direction to this outset of life.',
  },
  'Outshine': {
    'id': 37606,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSHI\'NE</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To send forth brightness or luster.<br><br>2. To excel in luster or excellence; as Homer outshines all other poets.',
  },
  'Outshoot': {
    'id': 37607,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSHOOT\'</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To exceed in shooting.<br><br>2. To shoot beyond.',
  },
  'Outshut': {
    'id': 37608,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSHUT\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To shut out or exclude.',
  },
  'Outside': {
    'id': 37609,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSI\'DE</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. The external part of a thing; the part, end or side which forms the surface or superficies.<br><br>2. Superficial appearance; exterior; as the outside of a man or of manners.<br><br>Created beings see nothing but our outside.<br><br>3. Person; external man.<br><br>4. The part or place that lies without or beyond an inclosure.<br><br>I threw open the door of my chamber and found the family standing on the outside.<br><br>5. The utmost.',
  },
  'Outsit': {
    'id': 37610,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSIT\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To sit beyond the time of any thing.',
  },
  'Outskip': {
    'id': 37611,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSKIP\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To avoid by flight.',
  },
  'Outskirt': {
    'id': 37612,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'SKIRT</b>, <i>n.</i> Border; outpost; suburb.',
  },
  'Outsleep': {
    'id': 37613,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSLEE\'P</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To sleep beyond.',
  },
  'Outsoar': {
    'id': 37614,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSOAR</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To soar beyond.',
  },
  'Outsound': {
    'id': 37615,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSOUND\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To surpass in sound.',
  },
  'Outspeak': {
    'id': 37616,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSPE\'AK</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To speak something beyond; to exceed.',
  },
  'Outsport': {
    'id': 37617,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSPORT</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To sport beyond; to outdo in sporting.',
  },
  'Outspread': {
    'id': 37618,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSPREAD\'</b>, <i>v.t</i> To extend; to spread; to diffuse.',
  },
  'Outstand': {
    'id': 37619,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSTAND\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i><br><br>1. To resist effectually; to withstand; to sustain without yielding. [Little used.]<br><br>2. To stand beyond the proper time.<br><br><b>OUTSTAND\'</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To project outwards from the main body.',
  },
  'Outstanding': {
    'id': 37620,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSTAND\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i><br><br>1. Resisting effectually. [Little used.]<br><br>2. Projecting outward.<br><br>3. Not collected; unpaid; as outstanding debts.<br><br>The whole amount of revenues - as well outstanding as collected.',
  },
  'Outstare': {
    'id': 37621,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSTA\'RE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To face down; to browbeat; to outface with effrontery; as we say, to stare out of countenance.',
  },
  'Outstep': {
    'id': 37622,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSTEP\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To step or go beyond; to exceed.',
  },
  'Outstorm': {
    'id': 37623,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSTORM\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To overbear by storming.<br><br>Insults the tempest and outstorms the skies.',
  },
  'Outstreet': {
    'id': 37624,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'STREET</b>, <i>n.</i> A street in the extremities of a town.',
  },
  'Outstretch': {
    'id': 37625,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSTRETCH\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To extend; to stretch or spread out; to expand.',
  },
  'Outstride': {
    'id': 37626,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSTRI\'DE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To surpass in striding.',
  },
  'Outstrip': {
    'id': 37627,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSTRIP\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To outgo; to outrun; to advance beyond.',
  },
  'Outswear': {
    'id': 37628,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSWEAR</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in swearing; to overpower by swearing.',
  },
  'Outsweeten': {
    'id': 37629,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSWEE\'TEN</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in sweetness.',
  },
  'Outswell': {
    'id': 37630,
    'definition': '<b>OUTSWELL\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To overflow; to exceed in swelling.',
  },
  'Outtalk': {
    'id': 37631,
    'definition': '<b>OUTTALK</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outtauk\'. To overpower by talking; to exceed in talking.',
  },
  'Outthrow': {
    'id': 37632,
    'definition': '<b>OUTTHROW</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To throw out or beyond.',
  },
  'Outtongue': {
    'id': 37633,
    'definition': '<b>OUTTONGUE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outtung\'. To bear down by talk, clamor or noise.',
  },
  'Outtop': {
    'id': 37634,
    'definition': '<b>OUTTOP\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To overtop. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Outvalue': {
    'id': 37635,
    'definition': '<b>OUTVAL\'UE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in price or value.',
  },
  'Outvenom': {
    'id': 37636,
    'definition': '<b>OUTVEN\'OM</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in poison.',
  },
  'Outvie': {
    'id': 37637,
    'definition': '<b>OUTVI\'E</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed; to surpass.',
  },
  'Outvillain': {
    'id': 37638,
    'definition': '<b>OUTVIL\'LAIN</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in villainy.',
  },
  'Outvoice': {
    'id': 37639,
    'definition': '<b>OUTVOICE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outvois\'. To exceed in roaring or clamor. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Outvote': {
    'id': 37640,
    'definition': '<b>OUTVO\'TE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in the number of votes given; to defeat by plurality of suffrages.',
  },
  'Outwalk': {
    'id': 37641,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWALK</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outwauk\'.<br><br>1. To walk faster than; to leave behind in walking.<br><br>2. To exceed the walking of a specter.',
  },
  'Outwall': {
    'id': 37642,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'WALL</b>, <i>n.</i><br><br>1. The exterior wall of a building or fortress.<br><br>2. Superficial appearance. [Unusual.]',
  },
  'Outward': {
    'id': 37643,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'WARD</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. versus.]<br><br>1. External; exterior; forming the superficial part; as the outward coat of an onion; an outward garment.<br><br>2. External; visible; opposed to inward; as outward hate.<br><br>3. Extrinsic; adventitious.<br><br>And outward honor for an inward toil.<br><br>4. Foreign; not intestine; as an outward war. [Not now used. We now say, external or foreign war.]<br><br>5. Tending to the exterior part.<br><br>The fire will force its outward way.<br><br>6. In Scripture, civil; public; as opposed to religious. 1 Chr 24.<br><br>7. In theology, carnal; fleshly; corporeal; not spiritual; as the outward man.<br><br><b>OUT\'WARD</b>, <i>n.</i> External form.',
  },
  'Outward-Bound': {
    'id': 37644,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWARD-BOUND\'</b>, <i>a.</i> Proceeding from a port or country.',
  },
  'Outwardly': {
    'id': 37645,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'WARDLY</b>, <i>adv.<br><br></i> 1. Externally; opposed to inwardly; as outwardly content, but inwardly uneasy.<br><br>2. In appearance; not sincerely. Many may inwardly reverence the goodness which they outwardly seem to despise.',
  },
  'Outwards': {
    'id': 37646,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'WARDS</b>, <i>adv.</i><br><br>1. To the outer parts; tending or directed towards the exterior.<br><br>The light falling on them [black bodies] is not reflected outwards.<br><br>2. From a port or country; as a ship bound outwards.',
  },
  'Outwash': {
    'id': 37647,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWASH\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To wash out; to cleanse from. [Little used.]',
  },
  'Outwatch': {
    'id': 37648,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWATCH\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To surpass in watching.',
  },
  'Outwear': {
    'id': 37649,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWEAR</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To wear out. [Not used.]<br><br>2. To pass tediously to the end.<br><br>By the stream, if I the night outwear -<br><br>3. To last longer than something else. [This is the common signification.]',
  },
  'Outweed': {
    'id': 37650,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWEE\'D</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To weed out; to extirpate, as a weed.',
  },
  'Outweep': {
    'id': 37651,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWEE\'P</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in weeping.',
  },
  'Outweigh': {
    'id': 37652,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWEIGH</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outwa\'y. [See <a href="dict://Weigh">Weigh</a>.]<br><br>1. To exceed in weight.<br><br>2. To exceed in value, influence or importance.<br><br>One self-approving hour whole years outweighs of stupid starers and of loud huzzas.',
  },
  'Outwell': {
    'id': 37653,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWELL\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> or i. To pour out. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Outwent': {
    'id': 37654,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWENT\'</b>, <i>pret.</i> of outgo.',
  },
  'Outwhore': {
    'id': 37655,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWHO\'RE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in lewdness.',
  },
  'Outwin': {
    'id': 37656,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWIN\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To get out of. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Outwind': {
    'id': 37657,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWIND</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To extricate by winding; to unloose.',
  },
  'Outwing': {
    'id': 37658,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWING\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To move faster on the wing; to outstrip.',
  },
  'Outwit': {
    'id': 37659,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWIT\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To surpass in design or stratagem; to overreach; to defeat or frustrate by superior ingenuity.',
  },
  'Outwork': {
    'id': 37660,
    'definition': '<b>OUT\'WORK</b>, <i>n.</i> The part of a fortification most remote from the main fortress or citadel.',
  },
  'Outworn': {
    'id': 37661,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWORN</b>, <i>pp.</i> [See <a href="dict://Wear">Wear</a>.] Worn out; consumed by use.',
  },
  'Outworth': {
    'id': 37662,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWORTH</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To exceed in value.',
  },
  'Outwrest': {
    'id': 37663,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWREST</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outrest\'. To extort; to draw from or forth by violence.',
  },
  'Outwrite': {
    'id': 37664,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWRITE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> outri\'te. To surpass in writing.',
  },
  'Outwrought': {
    'id': 37665,
    'definition': '<b>OUTWROUGHT</b>, <i>pp.</i> outraut\'. [See <a href="dict://Work">Work</a>.] Outdone; exceeded in act or efficacy.',
  },
  'Outzany': {
    'id': 37666,
    'definition': '<b>OUTZA\'NY</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [See <a href="dict://Zany">Zany</a>.] To exceed in buffoonery.',
  },
  'Oval': {
    'id': 37667,
    'definition': '<b>O\'VAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. ovum, an egg.]<br><br>1. Of the shape or figure of an egg; oblong; curvilinear; resembling the longitudinal section of an egg. It is sometimes synonymous with elliptical; but an ellipsis is equally broad at both ends, and is not strictly egg-shaped.<br><br>2. Pertaining to eggs; done in the egg; as oval conceptions.<br><br><b>O\'VAL</b>, <i>n.</i> A body or figure in the shape of an egg.',
  },
  'Ovarious': {
    'id': 37668,
    'definition': '<b>OVA\'RIOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> Consisting of eggs; as ovarious food.',
  },
  'Ovary': {
    'id': 37669,
    'definition': '<b>O\'VARY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. ovarium, from ovum, an egg.]<br><br>The part of a female animal in which the eggs are formed or lodged; or the part in which the fetus is supposed to be formed.',
  },
  'Ovate': {
    'id': 37670,
    'definition': '<b>O\'VATE,<br><br>OVATE-LAN\'CEOLATE</b>, <i>a.</i> Having something of the form of an egg and a lance, inclining to the latter.',
  },
  'Ovate-Subulate': {
    'id': 37671,
    'definition': '<b>OVATE-SUB\'ULATE</b>, <i>a.</i> Having something of the form of an egg and an awl, but most tending to the latter.',
  },
  'Ovated': {
    'id': 37672,
    'definition': '<b>O\'VATED</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. ovatus, from ovum, an egg.] Egg-shaped; as an ovate leaf.',
  },
  'Ovation': {
    'id': 37673,
    'definition': '<b>OVA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. ovatio.] In Roman antiquity, a lesser triumph allowed to commanders who had conquered without blood, or defeated an inconsiderable enemy.',
  },
  'Ovato-Oblong': {
    'id': 37674,
    'definition': '<b>OVATO-OB\'LONG</b>, <i>a.</i> Oblong in the shape of an egg, or with the end lengthened.',
  },
  'Oven': {
    'id': 37675,
    'definition': '<b>OVEN</b>, <i>n.</i> uv\'n.<br><br>An arch of brick or stone work, for baking bread and other things for food. Ovens are made in chimneys or set in the open air.',
  },
  'Over': {
    'id': 37676,
    'definition': '<b>O\'VER</b>, <i>prep.</i> [L. super., Gr.]<br><br>1. Across; from side to side; implying a passing or moving either above the substance or thing, or on the surface of it. Thus we say, a dog leaps over a stream, or over a table; a boat sails over a lake.<br><br>2. Above in place or position; opposed to below; as the clouds over our heads. The smoke rises over the city.<br><br>The mercy-seat that is over the testimony. Exo 30.<br><br>3. Above, denoting superiority in excellence, dignity or value; as the advantages which the christian world has over the heathen.<br><br>Young Pallas shone conspicuous o\'er the rest.<br><br>4. Above in authority, implying the right or power of superintending or governing; opposed to under.<br><br>Thou shalt be over my house. Gen 41.<br><br>I will make thee ruler over many things. Mat 25.<br><br>5. Upon the surface or whole surface; through the whole extent; as, to wander over the earth; to walk over a field, or over a city.<br><br>6. Upon. Watch over your children.<br><br>Dost thou not watch over my sin? Job 14.<br><br>His tender mercies are over all his works. Psa 145.<br><br>7. During the whole time; from beginning to end; as, to keep any thing over night; to keep corn over winter.<br><br>8. Above the top; covering; immersing; as, the water is over the shoes or boots.<br><br>Over night. In this phrase, over sometimes signifies before; as, when preparing for a journey, we provide things necessary over night.<br><br>Over, in poetry, is often contracted into o\'er.<br><br><b>O\'VER</b>, <i>adv.<br><br></i> 1. From side to side; as a board a foot over; a tree a foot over, a foot in diameter.<br><br>2. On the opposite side. The boat is safe over.<br><br>3. From one to another by passing; as, to deliver over goods to another.<br><br>4. From one country to another by passing; as, to carry any thing over to France, or to bring any thing over to England.<br><br>5. On the surface.<br><br>6. Above the top.<br><br>Good measure, pressed down and shaken together, and running over, shall men give into your bosom. Luke 6.<br><br>7. More than the quantity assigned; beyond a limit.<br><br>He that gathered much had nothing over. Exo 14.<br><br>8. Throughout; from beginning to end; completely; as, to read over a book; to argue a question over again.<br><br>Over and over, repeatedly; once and again.<br><br>And every night review\'d it o\'er and o\'er.<br><br>Over again, once more; with repetition.<br><br>O kill not all my kindred o\'er again.<br><br>Over and above, besides; beyond what is supposed or limited.<br><br>He gained, over and above, the good will of the people.<br><br>Over against, opposite; in front.<br><br>Over against this church stands a large hospital.<br><br>Over is used with rolling or turning from side to side; as, to turn over; to roll over.<br><br>1. To give over, to cease from; as, to give over an enterprize.<br><br>2. To consider as in a hopeless state; as, the physicians have given over their patient.<br><br>Over, in composition, denotes spreading, covering above; as in overcast, overflow; or across, as to overhear; or above, as to overhang; or turning, changing sides, as in overturn; or more generally beyond, implying excess or superiority, as in overact, overcome.<br><br><b>O\'VER</b>, <i>a.</i><br><br>1. Past.<br><br>The Olympic games were over.<br><br>2. Upper; covering; as over-shoes; over-leather.',
  },
  'Overabound': {
    'id': 37677,
    'definition': '<b>OVERABOUND\'</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To abound more than enough; to be superabundant.',
  },
  'Overact': {
    'id': 37678,
    'definition': '<b>OVERACT\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To act or perform to excess; as, he overacted his part.<br><br><b>OVERACT\'</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To act more than is necessary.',
  },
  'Overagitate': {
    'id': 37679,
    'definition': '<b>OVERAG\'ITATE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To agitate or discuss beyond what is expedient.',
  },
  'Overalls': {
    'id': 37680,
    'definition': '<b>O\'VERALLS</b>, <i>n.</i> A kind of trousers.',
  },
  'Overanxious': {
    'id': 37681,
    'definition': '<b>OVERANX\'IOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> Anxious to excess.',
  },
  'Overarch': {
    'id': 37682,
    'definition': '<b>OVER\'ARCH</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To arch over; to cover with an arch.<br><br>Brown with o\'erarching shades.',
  },
  'Overawe': {
    'id': 37683,
    'definition': '<b>OVERAWE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> overaw\'. To restrain by awe, fear or superior influence.<br><br>The kind was present in person to overlook that magistrates and overawe the subjects with the terror of his sword.',
  },
  'Overbalance': {
    'id': 37684,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBAL\'ANCE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To weigh down; to exceed in weight, value or importance. The evils which spring from vice overbalance all its pleasures.<br><br><b>OVERBAL\'ANCE</b>, <i>n.</i> Excess of weight or value; something more than an equivalent; as an overbalance of exports; an overbalance of probabilities.',
  },
  'Overbattle': {
    'id': 37685,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBAT\'TLE</b>, <i>a.</i><br><br>Too fruitful; exuberant. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Overbear': {
    'id': 37686,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBEAR</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [See <a href="dict://Bear">Bear</a>.] To bear down; to repress; to subdue.<br><br>The point of reputation, when the news first came of the battle lost, did overbear the reason of war.<br><br>Yet fortune, valor, all is overborne by numbers.<br><br>Till overborne with weight the Cyprians fell.',
  },
  'Overbearing': {
    'id': 37687,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBEARING</b>, <i>ppr.<br><br></i> 1. Bearing down; repressing.<br><br>2. a. Haughty and dogmatical; disposed or tending to repress or subdue by insolence or effrontery.',
  },
  'Overbend': {
    'id': 37688,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBEND\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To bend or stretch to excess.',
  },
  'Overbid': {
    'id': 37689,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBID\'</b>, <i>v.t.</i><br><br>1. To bid or offer beyond.<br><br>2. To bid or offer more than an equivalent.',
  },
  'Overblow': {
    'id': 37690,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBLOW</b>, <i>v.i.</i><br><br>1. To blow with too much violence; a seaman\'s phrase.<br><br>2. To blow over, or be past its violence. [Not used.]<br><br><b>OVERBLOW</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To blow away; to dissipate by wind.',
  },
  'Overblown': {
    'id': 37691,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBLOWN</b>, <i>pp.</i> Blown by and gone; blown away; driven by; past.<br><br>And when this cloud of sorrow\'s overblown.',
  },
  'Overboard': {
    'id': 37692,
    'definition': '<b>OVERBOARD</b>, <i>adv.</i> Literally, over the side of a ship; hence, out of a ship or from on board; as, to fall overboard; which of course is to fall into the water.',
  },
