  'Reap': {
    'id': 43478,
    'definition': '<b>REAP</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. rapio, carpo; Gr. a sickle, to reap; Eng. crop.]<br><br>1. To cut grain with a sickle; as, to reap wheat or rye.<br><br>When ye reap the harvest, thou shalt not wholly reap the corners of thy field. Lev 19.<br><br>2. To clear of a crop by reaping; as, to reap a field.<br><br>3. To gather; to obtain; to receive as a reward, or as the fruit of labor or of works; in a good or bad sense; as, to reap a benefit from exertions.<br><br>He that soweth to the flesh, shall of the flesh reap corruption. Gal 6.<br><br>Ye have plowed wickedness; ye have reaped iniquity.<br><br>Hosea 10.<br><br><b>REAP</b>, <i>v.i.<br><br></i> 1. To perform the act or operation of reaping. In New England, farmers reap in July and August.<br><br>2. To receive the fruit of labor or works.<br><br>They that sow in tears, shall reap in joy. Psa 126.',
  },
  'Reaped': {
    'id': 43479,
    'definition': '<b>RE\'APED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Cut with a sickle; received as the fruit of labor or works.',
  },
  'Reaper': {
