    'definition': '<b>REDEM\'ANDABLE</b>, <i>a.</i> That may be demanded back.',
  },
  'Redemanded': {
    'id': 43937,
    'definition': '<b>REDEM\'ANDED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Demanded back or again.',
  },
  'Redemanding': {
    'id': 43938,
    'definition': '<b>REDEM\'ANDING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Demanding back or again.',
  },
  'Redemise': {
    'id': 43939,
    'definition': '<b>REDEMI\'SE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> s as z. [re and demise.] To convey or transfer back, as an estate in fee simple, fee tail, for life or a term of years.<br><br><b>REDEMI\'SE</b>, <i>n.</i> Reconveyance; the transfer of an estate back to the person who has demised it; as the demise and redemise of an estate in fee simple, fee tail, or for life or years, by mutual leases.',
  },
  'Redemised': {
    'id': 43940,
    'definition': '<b>REDEMI\'SED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Reconveyed, as an estate.',
  },
  'Redemising': {
    'id': 43941,
    'definition': '<b>REDEMI\'SING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Reconveying.',
  },
  'Redemption': {
    'id': 43942,
    'definition': '<b>REDEMP\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. redemptio. See <a href="dict://Redeem">Redeem</a>.]<br><br>1. Repurchase of captured goods or prisoners; the act of procuring the deliverance of persons or things from the possession and power of captors by the payment of an equivalent; ransom; release; as the redemption of prisoners taken in war; the redemption of a ship and cargo.<br><br>2. Deliverance from bondage, distress, or from liability to any evil or forfeiture, either by money, labor or other means.<br><br>3. Repurchase, as of lands alienated. Lev 25. Jer 32.<br><br>4. The liberation of an estate from a mortgage; or the purchase of the right to re-enter upon it by paying the principal sum for which it was mortgaged with interest and cost; also, the right of redeeming and re-entering.<br><br>5. Repurchase of notes, bills or other evidence of debt by paying their value in specie to their holders.<br><br>6. In theology, the purchase of God\'s favor by the death and sufferings of Christ; the ransom or deliverance of sinners from the bondage of sin and the penalties of God\'s violated law by the atonement of Christ.<br><br>In whom we have redemption through his blood. Eph 1.<br><br>Col 1.',
  },
  'Redemptioner': {
    'id': 43943,
    'definition': '<b>REDEMP\'TIONER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who redeems himself, or purchases his release from debt or obligation to the master of a ship by his services; or one whose services are sold to pay the expenses of his passage to America.',
  },
  'Redemptory': {
    'id': 43944,
    'definition': '<b>REDEMP\'TORY</b>, <i>a.</i> Paid for ransom; as Hector\'s redemptory price.',
  },
  'Redented': {
    'id': 43945,
    'definition': '<b>REDENT\'ED</b>, <i>a.</i> Formed like the teeth of a saw; indented.',
  },
  'Redescend': {
    'id': 43946,
    'definition': '<b>REDESCEND\'</b>, <i>v.i.</i> [re and descent.] To descend again.',
