    'definition': '<b>SOPE</b>, [See <a href="dict://Soap">Soap</a>.]',
  },
  'Soph': {
    'id': 49577,
    'definition': '<b>SOPH</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. sophista.] In colleges and universities, a student in his second year; a sophomore.',
  },
  'Sophi': {
    'id': 49578,
    'definition': '<b>SO\'PHI</b>, <i>n.</i> A title of the king of Persia.',
  },
  'Sophical': {
    'id': 49579,
    'definition': '<b>SOPH\'ICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> Teaching wisdom. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Sophism': {
    'id': 49580,
    'definition': '<b>SOPH\'ISM</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. sophisma.] A specious but fallacious argument; asubtilty in reasoning; an argument that is not supported by sound reasoning , or in which the inference is not justly deduced from the premises. When a false argument puts on the appearance of a true one, then it is properly called a sophism or fallacy.',
  },
  'Sophist': {
    'id': 49581,
    'definition': '<b>SOPH\'IST</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. sophista.]<br><br>1. A professor of philosophy; as the sophists of Greece.<br><br>2. A captious or fallacious reasoner.',
  },
  'Sophister': {
    'id': 49582,
    'definition': '<b>SOPH\'ISTER</b>, <i>n.</i> [supra.]<br><br>1. A disputant fallaciously subtil; an artful but insidious logician; as an atheistical sophister. Not all the subtil objection of sophisters and rabbies against the gospel, so much prejudiced the reception of it, as the reproach of those crimes with which they aspersed the assemblies of Christians.<br><br>2. A professor of philosophy; a sophist.<br><br><b>SOPH\'ISTER</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To maintain by a fallacious argument. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Sophistic': {
    'id': 49583,
    'definition': '<b>SOPHIST\'IC, SOPHIST\'ICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> Fallaciously subtil; not sound; as sophistical reasoning or argument.',
  },
  'Sophistical': {
    'id': 49584,
    'definition': '<b>SOPHIST\'IC, SOPHIST\'ICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> Fallaciously subtil; not sound; as sophistical reasoning or argument.',
  },
  'Sophistically': {
    'id': 49585,
    'definition': '<b>SOPHIST\'ICALLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> With fallacious subtilty.',
  },
  'Sophisticate': {
    'id': 49586,
    'definition': '<b>SOPHIST\'ICATE</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To adulterate; to corrupt by something spurious or foreign; to pervert; as, to sophisticate nature, philosophy or the understanding.<br><br>2. To adulterate; to render spurious; as merchandise; as, to sophisticate wares or liquors. They purchase but sophisticated ware.<br><br><b>SOPHIST\'ICATE</b>, <i>a.</i> Adulterated; not pure; not genuine. So truth, when only one supplied the state, grew scarce and dear, and hey sophisticate.',
  },
  'Sophistication': {
    'id': 49587,
    'definition': '<b>SOPHISTICA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of adulterating; a counterfeiting or debasing the purity of some thing be a foreign admixture; adulteration.',
  },
  'Sophisticator': {
    'id': 49588,
    'definition': '<b>SOPHIST\'ICATOR</b>, <i>n.</i> One that adulterates; one who injures the purity and genuineness of any thing by foreign admixture.',
  },
  'Sophistry': {
    'id': 49589,
    'definition': '<b>SOPH\'ISTRY</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. Fallacious reasoning; reasoning sound in appearance only. These men have obscured and confounded the nature of things by their false principles and wretched sophistry.<br><br>2. Exercise in logic.',
