  'Spigot': {
    'id': 49972,
    'definition': '<b>SPIG\'OT</b>, <i>n.</i> A pin or peg used to stop a faucet, or to stop a small hole in a cask of liquor.',
  },
  'Spike': {
    'id': 49973,
    'definition': '<b>SPIKE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. L. spica, and ear of corn. It signifies a shoot or point.]<br><br>1. A large uail; always in American applied to a nail or pin of metal. A similar thing made of word is called a peg or pin. In England, it is sometimes used for a sharp point of wood.<br><br>2. An ear of corn or grain. It is applied to the heads of wheat, rye and barley; and is particularly applicable to the ears of maiz.<br><br>3. A shoot.<br><br>4. [L. spica.] In botany, a species of inflorescence, in which sessile flowers are alternate on a common simple peduncle, as in wheat and rye, lavender, &c.<br><br><b>SPIKE</b>, <i>n.</i> A smaller species of lavender.<br><br><b>SPIKE</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To fasten with spikes or long and large nails; as, to spike down the planks of a floor or bridge.<br><br>2. To set with spikes. A youth leaping over the spiked pales-was caught by the spikes. [Unusual.]<br><br>3. To stop the vent with spikes; as, to spike cannon.',
  },
  'Spike-Lavender': {
    'id': 49974,
    'definition': '<b>SPIKE-LAVENDER</b>, <i>n.</i> The Lavandula spica.',
  },
  'Spiked': {
    'id': 49975,
    'definition': '<b>SPI\'KED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Furnished with spikes, as corn; fastened with spikes; stopped with spikes.',
  },
  'Spikelet': {
    'id': 49976,
    'definition': '<b>SPI\'KELET</b>, <i>n.</i> In botany, a small spike of a large one; or a subdivision of a spike.',
