  'Tranters': {
    'id': 54515,
    'definition': '<b>TRAN\'TERS</b>, <i>n.</i> plu. Men who carry fish from the sea coast to sell in the inland countries. [Not American.]',
  },
  'Trap': {
    'id': 54516,
    'definition': '<b>TRAP</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. An engine that shuts suddenly or with a spring, used for taking game; as a trap for foxes. A trap is a very different thing from a snare; though the latter word may be used in a figurative sense for a trap.<br><br>2. An engine for catching men. [Not used in the U. States.]<br><br>3. An ambush; a stratagem; any device by which men or other animals may be caught unawares.<br><br>Let their table be made a snare and a trap. Rom 11.<br><br>4. A play in which a ball is driven with a stick.<br><br><b>TRAP</b>, <i>n.</i> In mineralogy, a name given to rocks characterized by a columnar form, or whose strata or beds have the form of steps or a series of stairs. Kirwan gives this name to two families of basalt. It is now employed to designate a rock or aggregate in which hornblend predominates, but it conveys no definite idea of any one species; and under this term are comprehended hornblend, hornblend slate, greenstone, greenstone slate, amygdaloid, basalt, wacky, clinkstone porphyry, and perhaps hypersthene rock, augite rock, and some varieties of sienite.<br><br><b>TRAP</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To catch in a trap; as, to trap foxes or beaver.<br><br>1. To ensnare; to take by stratagem.<br><br>I trapp\'d the foe.<br><br>2. To adorn; to dress with ornaments. [See <a href="dict://Trappings">Trappings</a>.] [the verb is little used.]<br><br><b>TRAP</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To set traps for game; as, to trap for beaver.',
  },
  'Trap-Tuff': {
    'id': 54517,
    'definition': '<b>TRAP\'-TUFF</b>, <i>n.</i> Masses of basalt, amygdaloid, hornblend, sandstones, &c., cemented.',
  },
  'Trapan': {
