  'Trijugous': {
    'id': 54741,
    'definition': '<b>TRIJU\'GOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres, three, and jugum, yoke.] In botany, having three pairs. A trijugous leaf is a pinnate leaf with three pairs of leaflets.',
  },
  'Trilateral': {
    'id': 54742,
    'definition': '<b>TRILAT\'ERAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres, three, and latus, side.] Having three sides.',
  },
  'Triliteral': {
    'id': 54743,
    'definition': '<b>TRILIT\'ERAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres, three, and litera, letter.] Consisting of three letters; as a triliteral root or word.<br><br><b>TRILIT\'ERAL</b>, <i>n.</i> A word consisting of three letters.',
  },
  'Trill': {
    'id': 54744,
    'definition': '<b>TRILL</b>, <i>n.</i> A quaver; a shake of the voice in singing, or of the sound of an instrument. [See <a href="dict://Shake">Shake</a>.]<br><br><b>TRILL</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To utter with a quavering or tremulousness of voice; to shake.<br><br>The sober-suited songstress trills her lay.<br><br><b>TRILL</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To flow in a small stream, or in drops rapidly succeeding each other; to trickle.<br><br>And now and then an ample tear trill\'d down<br><br>Her delicate cheek.<br><br>1. To shake or quaver; to play in tremulous vibrations of sound.<br><br>To judge of trilling notes and tripping feet.',
  },
  'Trilled': {
    'id': 54745,
    'definition': '<b>TRILL\'ED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Shaken; uttered with rapid vibrations.',
  },
  'Trilling': {
    'id': 54746,
    'definition': '<b>TRILL\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Uttering with a quavering or shake.',
  },
  'Trillion': {
    'id': 54747,
    'definition': '<b>TRILLION</b>, <i>n.</i> tril\'yun. [a word formed arbitrarily of three, or Gr. million.] The product of a million multiplied by a million, and that product multiplied by a million; or the product of the square of a million multiplied by a million. Thus 1,000,000 x 1,000,000=1,000,000,000,000, and this product multiplied by a million= 1,000,000,000,000,000,000.',
  },
  'Trilobate': {
    'id': 54748,
    'definition': '<b>TRILO\'BATE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and locus.] Having three lobes.',
  },
  'Trilocular': {
    'id': 54749,
    'definition': '<b>TRILOC\'ULAR</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and locus, a cell.] In botany, three-celled; having three cells for seeds; as a trilocular capsule.',
  },
  'Triluminar': {
    'id': 54750,
    'definition': '<b>TRILU\'MINAR<br><br>TRILU\'MINOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and lumen, light.] Having three lights.',
  },
  'Trim': {
    'id': 54751,
    'definition': '<b>TRIM</b>, <i>a.</i> Firm; compact; tight; snug; being in good order. We say of a ship, she is trim, or trim-built; every thing about the man is trim. We say of a person, he is trim, when his body is well shaped and firm; and we say, his dress is trim, when it sits closely to his body and appears tight and snug; and of posture we say, a man or a soldier is trim, when he stands erect. It is particularly applicable to soldiers, and in Saxon, truma is a troop or body of soldiers.<br><br><b>TRIM</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. In a general sense, to make right, that is, to put in due order for any purpose.<br><br>The hermit trimm\'d his little fire.<br><br>2. To dress; to put the body in a proper state.<br><br>I was trimm\'d in Julia\'s gown.<br><br>3. To decorate; to invest or embellish with extra ornaments; as, to trim a gown with lace.<br><br>4. To clip, as the hair of the head; also, to shave; that is, to put in due order.<br><br>5. To lop, as superfluous branches; to prune; as, to trim trees.<br><br>6. To supply with oil; as, to trim a lamp.<br><br>7. To make neat; to adjust.<br><br>I found her trimming up the diadem<br><br>On her dead mistress--<br><br>8. In carpentry, to dress, as timber; to make smooth.<br><br>9. To adjust the cargo of a ship, or the weight of persons or goods in a boat, so equally on each side of the center and at each end, that she shall sit well on the water and sail well. Thus we say, to trim a ship or a boat.<br><br>10. To rebuke; to reprove sharply; a popular use of the word.<br><br>11. To arrange in due order for sailing; as, to trim the sails.<br><br>To trim in, in carpentry, to fit, as a piece of timber into other work.<br><br>To trip up, to dress; to put in order.<br><br><b>TRIM</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To balance; to fluctuate between parties, so as to appear to favor each.<br><br><b>TRIM</b>, <i>n.</i> Dress; gear; ornaments.<br><br>1. The state of a ship or her cargo, ballast, masts, &c., by which she is well prepared for sailing.<br><br>Trim of the masts, is their position in regard to the ship and to each other, as near or distant,far forward or much aft, erect or raking.<br><br>Trim of sails, is that position and arrangement which is best adapted to impel the ship forward.',
  },
  'Trimeter': {
    'id': 54752,
    'definition': '<b>TRIM\'ETER</b>, <i>n.</i> A poetical division of verse, consisting of three measures.<br><br><b>TRIM\'ETER',
  },
  'Trimetrical': {
    'id': 54753,
    'definition': '<b>TRIMET\'RICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr. three measures.] Consisting of three poetical measures, forming an iambic of six feet.',
  },
  'Trimly': {
    'id': 54754,
    'definition': '<b>TRIM\'LY</b>, <i>adv.</i> Nicely; neatly; in good order.',
  },
  'Trimmed': {
    'id': 54755,
    'definition': '<b>TRIM\'MED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Put in good order; dressed; ornamented; clipped; shaved; balanced; rebuked.',
  },
  'Trimmer': {
    'id': 54756,
    'definition': '<b>TRIM\'MER</b>, <i>n.</i> One that trims; a timeserver.<br><br>1. A piece of timber fitted in.<br><br>All the joists and the trimmers for the staircase--',
  },
  'Trimming': {
    'id': 54757,
    'definition': '<b>TRIM\'MING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Putting in due order; dressing; decorating; pruning; balancing; fluctuating between parties.<br><br><b>TRIM\'MING</b>, <i>n.</i> Ornamental appendages to a garment, as lace, ribbons and the like.',
  },
  'Trimness': {
    'id': 54758,
    'definition': '<b>TRIM\'NESS</b>, <i>n.</i> Neatness; snugness; the state of being close and in good order.',
  },
  'Trinal': {
    'id': 54759,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'NAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. trinus, three.] Threefold.',
  },
  'Trine': {
    'id': 54760,
    'definition': '<b>TRINE</b>, <i>a.</i> Threefold; as trine dimension, that is, length, breadth and thickness.<br><br><b>TRINE</b>, <i>n.</i> [supra.] In astrology, the aspect of planets distant from each other 120 degrees, forming the figure of a trigon or triangle.<br><br><b>TRINE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To put in the aspect of a trine.',
  },
  'Trinervate': {
    'id': 54761,
    'definition': '<b>TRINERV\'ATE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and nervus.] In botany, having three nerves or unbranched vessels meeting behind or beyond the base.',
  },
  'Trinerve': {
    'id': 54762,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'NERVE<br><br>TRI\'NERVED</b>, <i>a.</i> In botany, a trinerved or three-nerved leaf, has three nerves or unbranched vessels meeting in the base of the leaf.',
  },
  'Tringle': {
    'id': 54763,
    'definition': '<b>TRIN\'GLE</b>, <i>n.</i> In architecture, a little square member or ornament, as a listel, reglet, platband and the like, but particularly a little member fixed exactly over every triglyph.',
  },
  'Trinitarian': {
    'id': 54764,
    'definition': '<b>TRINITA\'RIAN</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to the Trinity, or to the doctrine of the Trinity.<br><br><b>TRINITA\'RIAN</b>, <i>n.</i> One who believes the doctrine of the Trinity.<br><br>1. One of an order of religious, who made it their business to redeem christians from infidels.',
  },
  'Trinity': {
    'id': 54765,
    'definition': '<b>TRIN\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. trinitas; tres and unus, unitas, one, unity.]<br><br>In theology, the union of three persons in one Godhead, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.<br><br>In my whole essay, there is not any thing like an objection against the Trinity.',
  },
  'Trinket': {
    'id': 54766,
    'definition': '<b>TRINK\'ET</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. A small ornament, as a jewel, a ring and the like.<br><br>2. A thing of little value; tackle; tools.',
  },
  'Trinomial': {
    'id': 54767,
    'definition': '<b>TRINO\'MIAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and nomen.] In mathematics, a trinomial root, is a root consisting of three parts, connected by the signs + or -. Thus x+y+z, or a+b-c.<br><br><b>TRINO\'MIAL</b>, <i>n.</i> A root of three terms or parts.',
  },
  'Trio': {
    'id': 54768,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'O</b>, <i>n.</i> A concert of three parts; three united.',
  },
  'Triobolar': {
    'id': 54769,
    'definition': '<b>TRIOB\'OLAR</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. triobolaris; tres and obolus.] Of the value of three oboli; mean; worthless. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Trioctahedral': {
    'id': 54770,
    'definition': '<b>TRIOCTAHE\'DRAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [tri and octahedral.] In crystallography, presenting three ranges of faces, one above another, each range containing eight faces.',
  },
  'Trioctile': {
    'id': 54771,
    'definition': '<b>TRIOC\'TILE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. tres, three, and octo, eight.] In astrology, an aspect of two planets with regard to the earth, when they are three octants or eight parts of a circle, that is, 135 degrees, distant from each other.',
  },
  'Trior': {
    'id': 54772,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'OR<br><br>TRI\'ER</b>, <i>n.</i> [from try.] In law, a person appointed by the court to examine whether a challenge to a panel of jurors, or to any juror, is just. The triors are two indifferent persons.',
  },
  'Trip': {
    'id': 54773,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP</b>, <i>v.t.<br><br></i> 1. To supplant; to cause to fall by striking the feet suddenly from under the person; usually followed by up; as, to trip up a man in wrestling; to trip up the heels.<br><br>2. To supplant; to overthrow by depriving of support.<br><br>3. To catch; to detect.<br><br>4. To loose an anchor from the bottom by its cable or buoy-rope.<br><br><b>TRIP</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To stumble; to strike the foot against something, so as to lose the step and come near to fall; or to stumble and fall.<br><br>1. To err; to fail; to mistake; to be deficient.<br><br>Virgil pretends sometimes to trip.<br><br><b>TRIP</b>, <i>v.i.<br><br></i> 1. To run or step lightly; to walk with a light step.<br><br>She bounded by and tripp\'d so light.<br><br>They had not time to take a steady sight.<br><br>Thus from the lion trips the trembling doe.<br><br>2. To take a voyage or journey.<br><br><b>TRIP</b>, <i>n.</i> A stroke or catch by which a wrestler supplants his antagonist.<br><br>And watches with a trip his foe to foil.<br><br>1. A stumble by the loss of foot-hold, or a striking of the foot against an object.<br><br>2. A failure; a mistake.<br><br>Each seeming trip, and each digressive start.<br><br>3. A journey; or a voyage.<br><br>I took a trip to London on the death of the queen.<br><br>4. In navigation, a single board in plying to windward.<br><br>5. Among farmers, a small flock of sheep, or a small stock of them. [Local.]',
  },
  'Tripartite': {
    'id': 54774,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'ARTITE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tripartitus; tres, three, and partitus, divided; partior.]<br><br>1. Divided into three parts. In botany, a tripartite leaf is one which is divided into three parts down to the base, but not wholly separate.<br><br>2. Having three corresponding parts or copies; as indentures tripartite.',
  },
  'Tripartition': {
    'id': 54775,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPARTI\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> A division by three, or the taking of a third part of any number or quantity.',
  },
  'Tripe': {
    'id': 54776,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPE</b>, <i>n.<br><br></i> 1. Properly, the entrails; but in common usage, the large stomach of ruminating animals, prepared for food.<br><br>2. In ludicrous language, the belly.',
  },
  'Tripe-Man': {
    'id': 54777,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'PE-MAN</b>, <i>n.</i> A man who sells tripe.',
  },
  'Tripedal': {
    'id': 54778,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'EDAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and pes.] Having three feet.',
  },
  'Tripennate': {
    'id': 54779,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPEN\'NATE<br><br>TRIPER\'SONAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and persona.] Consisting of three persons.',
  },
  'Tripersonality': {
    'id': 54780,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPERSONAL\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> The state of existing in three persons in one Godhead.',
  },
  'Tripetalous': {
    'id': 54781,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPET\'ALOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr. three, and leaf.] In botany, three-petaled; having three petals or flower leaves.',
  },
  'Triphane': {
    'id': 54782,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'PHANE</b>, <i>n.</i> A mineral, spodumene.',
  },
  'Triphthong': {
    'id': 54783,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPH\'THONG</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. three, and sound.] A coalition of three vowels in one compound sound, or in one syllable, as in adieu, eye.',
  },
  'Triphthongal': {
    'id': 54784,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPHTHON\'GAL</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to a triphthong; consisting of a triphthong.',
  },
  'Triphyllous': {
    'id': 54785,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPH\'YLLOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr. three, and leaf.] In botany, three-leaved; having three leaves.',
  },
  'Tripinnate': {
    'id': 54786,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPIN\'NATE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and penna or pinna.] In botany, a tripinnate leaf is a species of superdecompound leaf, when a petiole has bipinnate leaves ranged on each side of it, as in common fern.',
  },
  'Triple': {
    'id': 54787,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'LE</b>, <i>a.[L.</i> triplex, triplus; tres and plico, to fold.]<br><br>1. Threefold; consisting of three united; as a triple knot; a triple tie.<br><br>By thy triple shape as thou are seen--<br><br>2. Treble; three times repeated. [See <a href="dict://Treble">Treble</a>.]<br><br>Triple time, in music, is that in which each bar is divided into three measures or equal parts, as three minims, three crotchets, three quavers, &c.<br><br><b>TRIP\'LE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> To treble; to make threefold or thrice as much or as many. [Usually written treble.]',
  },
  'Triplet': {
    'id': 54788,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'LET</b>, <i>n.</i> [from triple.] Three of a kind, or three united.<br><br>1. In poetry, three verses rhyming together.<br><br>2. In music, three notes sung or played in the time of two.',
  },
  'Triplicate': {
    'id': 54789,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'LICATE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. triplicatus, triplico; tres and plico, to fold.] Made thrice as much; threefold.<br><br>Triplicate ratio, is the ratio which cubes bear to each other.',
  },
  'Triplication': {
    'id': 54790,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPLICA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of trebling or making threefold, or adding three together.<br><br>1. In the civil law, the same as sur-rejoinder in common law.',
  },
  'Triplicity': {
    'id': 54791,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPLIC\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. triplex.] Trebleness; the state of being threefold.',
  },
  'Triply-Ribbed': {
    'id': 54792,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'LY-RIBBED</b>, <i>a.</i> [triple and rib.] In botany, having a pair of large ribs branching off from the main one above the base, as in the leaves of many species of sunflower.<br><br><b>TRIP\'-MADAM</b>, <i>n.</i> A plant.',
  },
  'Tripod': {
    'id': 54793,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'POD</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. tripus, tripodis; Gr. three, and foot.]<br><br>A bench, stool or seat supported by three legs, on which the priest and sibyls in ancient times were placed to render oracles.',
  },
  'Tripoli': {
    'id': 54794,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'OLI</b>, <i>n.</i> In mineralogy, a mineral originally brought from Tripoli, used in polishing stones and metals. It has a dull argillaceous appearance, but is not compact. It has a fine hard grain, but does not soften by water, or mix with it. It is principally composed of silex.',
  },
  'Tripoline': {
    'id': 54795,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'OLINE</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to tripoli.',
  },
  'Tripos': {
    'id': 54796,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'POS</b>, <i>n.</i> A tripod, which see.',
  },
  'Tripped': {
    'id': 54797,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'PED</b>, <i>pp.</i> [from trip.] Supplanted.',
  },
  'Tripper': {
    'id': 54798,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'PER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who trips or supplants; one that walks nimbly.',
  },
  'Tripping': {
    'id': 54799,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'PING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Supplanting; stumbling; falling; stepping nimbly.<br><br>1. a. Quick; nimble.<br><br><b>TRIP\'PING</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of tripping.<br><br>1. A light dance.<br><br>2. The loosing of an anchor from the ground by its cable or buoy-rope.',
  },
  'Trippingly': {
    'id': 54800,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'PINGLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> Nimbly; with a light nimble quick step; with agility.<br><br>Sing and dance it trippingly.<br><br>Speak the speech trippingly on the tongue.',
  },
  'Triptote': {
    'id': 54801,
    'definition': '<b>TRIP\'TOTE</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. three, and case.] In grammar, a name having three cases only.',
  },
  'Tripudiary': {
    'id': 54802,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPU\'DIARY</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tripudium.] Pertaining to dancing; performed by dancing.',
  },
  'Tripudiation': {
    'id': 54803,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPUDIA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. tripudio, to dance.] Act of dancing.',
  },
  'Tripyramid': {
    'id': 54804,
    'definition': '<b>TRIPYR\'AMID</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. tres and pyramis.] In mineralogy, a genus of spars, the body of which is composed of single pyramids, each of three sides, affixed by their base to some solid body.',
  },
  'Triquetrous': {
    'id': 54805,
    'definition': '<b>TRIQUE\'TROUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. triquetrus, from triquetra, a triangle.]<br><br>Three-sided; having three plane sides.',
  },
  'Triradiated': {
    'id': 54806,
    'definition': '<b>TRIRA\'DIATED</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and radius.] Having three rays.',
  },
  'Trireme': {
