    'definition': '<b>TRISECT\'ING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Dividing into three equal parts.',
  },
  'Trisection': {
    'id': 54814,
    'definition': '<b>TRISEC\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. tres and sectio, a cutting.] The division of a thing into three parts; particularly in geometry, the division of an angle into three equal parts.',
  },
  'Trisepalous': {
    'id': 54815,
    'definition': '<b>TRISEP\'ALOUS</b>, <i>a.</i> In botany, having three sepals to a calyx.',
  },
  'Trispast': {
    'id': 54816,
    'definition': '<b>TRIS\'PAST<br><br>TRISPAS\'TON</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. to draw.] In mechanics, a machine with three pulleys for raising great weights.',
  },
  'Trispermous': {
    'id': 54817,
    'definition': '<b>TRISPERM\'OUS</b>, <i>a.</i> [Gr. three, and seed.] Three-seeded; containing three seeds; as a trispermous capsule.',
  },
  'Trist': {
    'id': 54818,
    'definition': '<b>TRIST<br><br>TRIST\'FUL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tristis, sad.] Sad; sorrowful; gloomy. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Trisulc': {
    'id': 54819,
    'definition': '<b>TRISULC\'</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. trisulcus.] Something having three points. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Trisyllabic': {
    'id': 54820,
    'definition': '<b>TRISYLLAB\'IC<br><br>TRISYLLAB\'ICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [from trisyllable.] Pertaining to a trisyllable; consisting of three syllables; as a trisyllabic word or root.',
  },
  'Trisyllable': {
    'id': 54821,
    'definition': '<b>TRISYL\'LABLE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. tres, three, and syllaba, syllable.] A word consisting of three syllables.',
  },
  'Tritely': {
    'id': 54822,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'TELY</b>, <i>adv.</i> In a common manner.',
  },
  'Triteness': {
    'id': 54823,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'TENESS</b>, <i>n.</i> Commonness; staleness; a state of being worn out; as the triteness of an observation or a subject.',
  },
  'Triternate': {
    'id': 54824,
    'definition': '<b>TRITERN\'ATE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres, three, and ternate.] Having three biternate leaves, or the divisions of a triple petiole subdivided into threes; a species of superdecompound leaf.',
  },
  'Tritheism': {
    'id': 54825,
    'definition': '<b>TRITHE\'ISM</b>, <i>n.</i> The opinion or doctrine that there are three Gods in the Godhead.',
  },
  'Tritheist': {
    'id': 54826,
    'definition': '<b>TRITHE\'IST</b>, <i>n.</i> One who believes that there are three distinct Gods in the Godhead, that is, three distinct substances, essences of hypostases.',
  },
  'Tritheistic': {
    'id': 54827,
    'definition': '<b>TRITHEIS\'TIC</b>, <i>a.</i> Pertaining to tritheism.',
  },
  'Tritheite': {
    'id': 54828,
    'definition': '<b>TRITHE\'ITE</b>, <i>n.</i> A tritheist.',
  },
  'Trithing': {
    'id': 54829,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'THING</b>, <i>n.</i> [from three.] One of the divisions of the county of York in England, which is divided into three parts. It is now called Riding.',
  },
  'Tritical': {
    'id': 54830,
    'definition': '<b>TRIT\'ICAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [from trite.] Trite; common. [Not in use.]',
  },
  'Triticalness': {
    'id': 54831,
    'definition': '<b>TRIT\'ICALNESS</b>, <i>n.</i> Triteness. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Triton': {
    'id': 54832,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'TON</b>, <i>n.</i> In mythology, a fabled sea demi-god, supposed to be the trumpeter of Neptune. He is represented by poets and painters as half man and half fish.<br><br>1. A genus of the molluscal order of worms.<br><br>2. A bird of the West Indies, famous for its notes.',
  },
  'Tritone': {
    'id': 54833,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'TONE</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. tres and tonus.] In music, a false concord, consisting of three tones,two major and one minor tone, or of two tones and two semitones; a dissonant interval.',
  },
  'Tritoxyd': {
    'id': 54834,
    'definition': '<b>TRITOX\'YD</b>, <i>n.</i> [Gr. third, and oxyd.] In chimistry, a substance oxydized in the third degree.',
  },
  'Triturable': {
    'id': 54835,
    'definition': '<b>TRIT\'URABLE</b>, <i>a.</i> [See <a href="dict://Triturate">Triturate</a>.] Capable of being reduced to a fine powder by pounding, rubbing or grinding.',
  },
  'Triturate': {
    'id': 54836,
    'definition': '<b>TRIT\'URATE</b>, <i>v.t.</i> [L. trituro, from tritus, tero, to wear.] To rub or grind to a very fine powder, and properly to a finer powder than that made by pulverization.',
  },
  'Triturated': {
    'id': 54837,
    'definition': '<b>TRIT\'URATED</b>, <i>pp.</i> Reduced to a very fine powder.',
  },
  'Triturating': {
    'id': 54838,
    'definition': '<b>TRIT\'URATING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Grinding or reducing to a very fine powder.',
  },
  'Trituration': {
    'id': 54839,
    'definition': '<b>TRITURA\'TION</b>, <i>n.</i> The act of reducing to a fine powder by grinding.',
  },
  'Triture': {
    'id': 54840,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'TURE</b>, <i>n.</i> A rubbing or grinding. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Triturium': {
    'id': 54841,
    'definition': '<b>TRITU\'RIUM</b>, <i>n.</i> A vessel for separating liquors of different densities.',
  },
  'Triumph': {
    'id': 54842,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'UMPH</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. triumphus.]<br><br>1. Among the ancient Romans, a pompous ceremony performed in honor of a victorious general, who was allowed to enter the city crowned, originally with laurel, but in later times with gold, bearing a truncheon in one hand and a branch of laurel in the other, riding in a chariot drawn by two white horses, and followed by the kings, princes and generals whom he had vanquished, loaded with chains and insulted by mimics and buffoons. The triumph was of two kinds, the greater and the less. The lesser triumph was granted for a victory over enemies of less considerable power, and was called an ovation.<br><br>2. State of being victorious.<br><br>Hercules from Spain<br><br>Arriv\'d in triumph, from Geryon slain.<br><br>3. Victory; conquest.<br><br>The vain coquets the trifling triumphs boast.<br><br>4. Joy or exultation for success.<br><br>Great triumph and rejoicing was in heav\'n.<br><br>5. A card that takes all others; now written trump, which see.<br><br><b>TRI\'UMPH</b>, <i>v.i.</i> To celebrate victory with pomp; to rejoice for victory.<br><br>How long shall the wicked triumph? Psa 94.<br><br>1. To obtain victory.<br><br>There fix thy faith, and triumph o\'er the world.<br><br>Attir\'d with stars, we shall forever sit<br><br>Triumphing over death.<br><br>2. In insult upon an advantage gained.<br><br>Let not my enemies triumph over me. Psa 25.<br><br>Sorrow on all the pack of you<br><br>That triumph thus upon my misery.<br><br>3. To be prosperous; to flourish.<br><br>Where commerce triumph\'d on the favoring gales.<br><br>triumph over,to succeed in overcoming; to surmount; as, to triumph over all obstacles.',
  },
  'Triumphal': {
    'id': 54843,
    'definition': '<b>TRIUMPH\'AL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. triumphalis.] Pertaining to triumph; used in a triumph; as a triumphal crown or car; a triumphal arch.<br><br><b>TRIUMPH\'AL</b>, <i>n.</i> A token of victory.',
  },
  'Triumphant': {
    'id': 54844,
    'definition': '<b>TRIUMPH\'ANT</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. triumphans.] Celebrating victory; as a triumphant chariot.<br><br>1. Rejoicing as for victory.<br><br>Successful beyond hope to lead you forth<br><br>Triumphant out of this infernal pit.<br><br>2. Victorious; graced with conquest.<br><br>So shall it be in the church triumphant.<br><br>Athena, war\'s triumphant maid--<br><br>3. Celebrating victory; expressing joy for success; as a triumphant song.',
  },
  'Triumphantly': {
    'id': 54845,
    'definition': '<b>TRIUMPH\'ANTLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> In a triumphant manner; with the joy and exultation that proceeds from victory or success.<br><br>Through armed ranks triumphantly she drives.<br><br>1. Victoriously; with success.<br><br>Triumphantly tread on thy country\'s ruin.<br><br>2. With insolent exultation.',
  },
  'Triumpher': {
    'id': 54846,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'UMPHER</b>, <i>n.</i> One who triumphs or rejoices for victory; one who vanquishes.<br><br>1. One who was honored with a triumph in Rome.',
  },
  'Triumphing': {
    'id': 54847,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'UMPHING</b>, <i>ppr.</i> Celebrating victory with pomp; vanquishing; rejoicing for victory; insulting on an advantage.',
  },
  'Triumvir': {
    'id': 54848,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'UMVIR</b>, <i>n.</i> [L. tres, three, and vir, man.] One of three men united in office. The triumvirs, L. triumviri, of Rome, were three men who jointly obtained the sovereign power in Rome. The first of these were Caesar, Crassus and Pompey.',
  },
  'Triumvirate': {
    'id': 54849,
    'definition': '<b>TRIUM\'VIRATE</b>, <i>a.</i> A coalition of three men; particularly, the union of three men who obtained the government of the Roman empire.<br><br>1. Government by three men in coalition.',
  },
  'Triune': {
    'id': 54850,
    'definition': '<b>TRI\'UNE</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. tres and unus.] Three in one; an epithet applied to God, to express the unity of the Godhead in a trinity of persons.',
  },
  'Triunity': {
    'id': 54851,
    'definition': '<b>TRIU\'NITY</b>, <i>n.</i> Trinity. [Not used.]',
  },
  'Trivalvular': {
    'id': 54852,
    'definition': '<b>TRIVALV\'ULAR</b>, <i>a.</i> Three-valved; having three valves.',
  },
  'Trivant': {
    'id': 54853,
    'definition': '<b>TRIV\'ANT</b>, <i>n.</i> A truant.',
  },
  'Triverbial': {
    'id': 54854,
    'definition': '<b>TRIVERB\'IAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. triverbium.] Triverbial days, in the Roman calendar, were juridical or court days, days allowed to the pretor for hearing causes; called also dies fasti. There were only twenty eight in the year.',
  },
  'Trivet': {
    'id': 54855,
    'definition': '<b>TRIV\'ET</b>, <i>n.</i> A three legged stool. [See <a href="dict://Trevet">Trevet</a>.]',
  },
  'Trivial': {
    'id': 54856,
    'definition': '<b>TRIV\'IAL</b>, <i>a.</i> [L. trivialis; probably from Gr.; L. tero, trivi, to wear, or from trivium, a highway.]<br><br>1. Trifling; of little worth or importance; inconsiderable; as a trivial subject; a trivial affair.<br><br>2. Worthless; vulgar.<br><br>Trivial name, in natural history, the common name for the species, which added to the generic name forms the complete denomination of the species; the specific name. Thus in Lathyrus aphaca, Lathyrus is the generic name, and aphaca the trivial or specific name, and the two combined form the complete denomination of the species. Linne at first applied the term specific name to the essential character of the species, now called the specific definition or difference; but it is now applied solely to the trivial name.',
  },
  'Triviality': {
    'id': 54857,
    'definition': '<b>TRIVIAL\'ITY</b>, <i>n.</i> Trivialness. [Not much used.]',
  },
  'Trivially': {
    'id': 54858,
    'definition': '<b>TRIV\'IALLY</b>, <i>adv.</i> Commonly; vulgarly.<br><br>1. Lightly; inconsiderably; in a trifling degree.',
